window.harnessChunks["rows-5d497f90fdc85ca7.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T00:44:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jonathanung/strike\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Strike</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jonathanung/strike/tree/0191bb896b5a1969c5b7b784597ed891b814234a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0191bb896b5a1969c5b7b784597ed891b814234a\">0191bb8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform coding, repository inspection, editing, shell/build/test work and local task-specific recovery against a changing workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous requirements, differing code paths, changing file state, failed tool/command results, and model/tool uncertainties while pursuing assigned outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-specific next model/tool action and revise the sequence after returned tool, workspace or child evidence. - Decision owner: lead model-backed session, or each child model-backed session for its own bounded operational objective. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Go engine loop, tool registry, permissions, file-state guards, session JSONL, budgets, scheduler, optional sandbox and provider adapters. - Closure path: user/subtask objective → model request/decision → first-party tool action → returned result and updated session state → later model decision or completion. - Boundary reachability: standard CLI/headless/engine composition builds and invokes this loop; child creation uses the same operational engine. No evaluation-only or contributor-only actor is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves a deterministic tool runner and control plumbing but no context-dependent choice among code edits, investigations and validations. - Evidence: harness/engine/engine.go; cmd/strike/assembletools.go; harness/engine/child.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no live model was invoked during this source-pinned assessment; tool permissions may block a chosen operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate conflicting work claims and path modifications among separately acting child/lead S1 sessions in one shared workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two live agents can claim the same work item, overwrite an actively held file/prefix, or submit mutually incompatible patches. - Distinct S1 units: lead and spawned child sessions with separate engine/model/tool action loops, and multiple concurrent children on the same implicit team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: live team members share a workspace and team task/patch state; duplicate task claims and conflicting file leases/touches are explicitly represented and rejected or warned by first-party code. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-facing teamtask uses owner-exclusive claim and CAS; agentownership supplies inspectable exclusive/shared leases; write touches and patchcollab expose overlaps, with session.overlapPolicy=block able to reject conflicting mutation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflict/claim results return to the invoking model, live path overlaps reach the team event stream, and ownership claims alter what other member sessions may subsequently claim/write under the configured policy. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: claim exclusivity and conflict-aware path leases target demonstrable shared-workspace collision modes of independent S1s rather than merely transferring or listing their tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise task claims, leases and coordinated file scopes in light of contested ownership and overlap feedback. - Decision owner: model-backed team actors choose claims/scopes and adjust behavior; deterministic shared board, path tracker, CAS and policy enforce or report conflicts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team shared state, team board, PathOwnership, agentownership, policy setting, file mutation hooks, patch conflict detection and event delivery. - Closure path: autonomous S1 requests claim/lease → conflict-aware team registry decides admissibility → return result/warning/block → same or competing S1 chooses a different task/path or waits; subsequent changes obey the updated claim. - Boundary reachability: cmd/strike/assembletools.go registers teamtask, agentownership and patchcollab in the shipped registry, while the standard child engine shares the team&#39;s state and file ownership tracker. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: code can reject a preset conflict, but without an autonomous actor it cannot decide which meaningful task/file scope should be taken next following a denial. - Evidence: harness/engine/teamboard.go; harness/tool/ownership.go; harness/tool/agentownership.go; harness/engine/teamcontroltest.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the default overlap policy warns rather than blocks file writes; stronger protection is a supported configuration. The claim board itself nevertheless enforces exclusive ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current subordinate commitments, resources, priorities and interventions on behalf of a live multi-child session team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independently evolving child work becomes blocked, stalled, over-budget, conflicting or obsolete relative to the lead&#39;s overall objective and remaining resources. - Whole-system current view: the model-facing agentroster exposes the complete session team with current status, objectives, last actions, blocks, touched files and budget/usage/stall fields; the progressive task list/status API tracks a shared delegation population, not just a single awaited handoff. - Current-control decision scope: lead can choose child assignments/personas/budgets and dependencies, subscribe to block/completion feedback, interrupt/cancel/reopen work and transition owned delegations; supported root human controls can separately spawn, interrupt and change delegation/board commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: revise live-team resource and commitment choices using current population observations, including exceptions and blocked/dependency state. - Decision owner: autonomous lead model in the base mode; human operator controlling the live root team via the shipped web-cockpit protocol in the distinct parent mode. Neither the scheduler nor the dashboard owns these choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: roster and task tools; child state/budget record; delegation lifecycle with CAS and dependency guards; shared scheduler; human team. Op permission/identity/root checks and browser UI. - Closure path: live team roster/delegation exception → lead model observes status and issues bounded task create/cancel/transition or allocation update → engine changes child commitments and reports state → continued child/lead action. In parent mode, live team observation → human operation through team.spawn, team.childinterrupt, team.tasktransition, or board Ops → engine applies/rejects and publishes updated team state. - Boundary reachability: the CLI registry exposes roster and progressive task controls to the lead; the first-party live web cockpit implements separate root-authorized human team-control Ops through the same engine, not an imagined plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the lead model from the autonomous mode leaves a status dashboard and rule enforcement but no task-specific cross-child intervention judgment. Parent mode makes that judgment explicitly human, with the same first-party return path. - Evidence: harness/engine/agentroster.go; harness/tool/task.go; harness/engine/delegation.go; harness/engine/teamcontrol.go; internal/frontend/server/api.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: claims concern one active session team; global scheduler limit config is enforcement, not agent-owned budget governance. The human path requires an active, mutation-authorized live root; historical/attach-only and read-only modes do not close it.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: offer a parent/host-controlled, implementer-independent completion challenge on a subordinate actor&#39;s claimed result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer can wrongly claim to have completed required work or tests; routine model handoff text may assert success without supporting reality. - Claim being audited: a child session&#39;s completed/self-reported verification and, optionally, a solo root&#39;s claimed final turn. - Ordinary reporting path: model-produced terminal handoff (summary, verification, files changed and findings) or ordinary tool results in the implementer&#39;s execution stream. - Complementary access path: a separately executing engine verify.Runner inspects actual filesystem path existence, independent shell-command outcomes and/or structured handoff schema, ignoring the model&#39;s self-certification string; a failed check changes a claimed-completed child to blocked and injects failures into handoff blockers/lead notification. - Independence boundary: the checks run after the child has claimed success under the parent engine, not as the child&#39;s own verification prose. However the shipped general component is a configured deterministic gate runner; its independent audit strategy, sampling, or model-based auditor is not a fully autonomous, separately assigned first-party role. - Who acts on findings: the parent engine blocks promotion of a failed child and feeds gate failures to the lead; the lead or operator can correct/re-delegate. Autonomous complementary audit judgment beyond the specified gates must be composed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which independent checks to require and interpret their direct-evidence failure, versus accepting the implementer claim. - Decision owner: the parent/host configuring verify gates owns the audit brief; the independent deterministic runner owns factual pass/fail evaluation and blocking, not autonomous audit policy or judgment in the general case. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harness/verify/verify.go, harness/engine/verify.go, child.go gate-to-blocked transition, verification events and handoff error propagation. - Closure path: implementer completion claim → independent parent-side gate execution → direct-evidence verdict → failed claim demoted to blocked and exposed to lead → bounded corrective response/redelegation. Constructor state reflects missing autonomous auditor/selection ownership rather than absence of this first-party function-specific path. - Boundary reachability: the shipping task API accepts verify gates, child.go invokes the independent runner, and the ordinary parent receives blocked/status evidence; this is not imported from the separate benchmark evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no model can override a failed gate by claiming it passed; the shipped checking judgment is deterministic and task-configured. The runtime offers the independent complementary witness, but does not itself provide an autonomous audit investigator beyond it. - Evidence: harness/verify/verify.go; harness/engine/verify.go; harness/engine/child.go; harness/engine/verifygatestest.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: opt-in gates cover defined outcomes and can resemble ordinary QA; C is credited only to the bounded first-party complementary check of implementer claims, not to evaluation/CI in the adjacent development organization or a claim of broad independent review.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct externally and prospectively oriented adaptation system that changes Strike&#39;s own present organizational capability was established at the assessed team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external web/documentation tools, code intelligence, agent/persona selection, repository memory, plan/workflow/goal loops and model routing can influence current work, but the reviewed paths do not form a prospective capability-adaptation conversation with S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for future environmental adaptation of the operating organization. - Decision owner: not established; agents can plan user tasks and operators can change static config, not thereby own S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web/browser tools, goal-loop planner/critic, scheduled loops, workflow phases, skill/persona discovery, model routing and local memory/ledger. - Closure path: no first-party external/future distinction → developed adaptation option → capability intervention returning into team-wide current S3 control was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current task research, local replanning, recurring prompts and failure memory remain current operations without first-party prospective strategic choice/return. - Evidence: docs/goal.md; docs/loop.md; docs/agents-skills.md. - Basis: explicit + bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: users can script ongoing market or technology research with tools, but that is not a first-party always-linked adaptation decision in the assessed organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision and returned governance loop exists at the assessed runtime team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission, sandbox, provider selection, workflow gates and human approval constrain tools and phases, but do not themselves adjudicate organizational identity or an S3–S4 policy tension. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no such ultimate-policy issue/decision right established at the team boundary. - Decision owner: developers/operators select settings and grant ordinary access; they do not constitute a documented first-party runtime S5 closure merely by doing so. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: managed permission denies, tool authorization, sandbox, config, team. human control, audit log, workflow /autonomy gate. - Closure path: no identity-level issue → legitimate ultimate authority → binding policy return into the same operating team established. Human team. acts on live work and is counted only as S3 parent control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither model nor operator is given a first-party process for resolving ultimate identity/policy questions; existing policies merely bound choices already assigned elsewhere. - Evidence: harness/engine/teamcontrol.go; docs/human-orchestration-ops.md; docs/usage.md. - Basis: explicit + bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: higher-level real human or OSS governance may exist outside this operational recursion, but co-location is not a first-party S5 product path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8d7a754a51eda30f9474bbce8d8d67dbcb56abd1/assessments/st/strike.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8d7a754a51eda30f9474bbce8d8d67dbcb56abd1\">8d7a754 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-28T16:51:52Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJU-REAL/HugAgentOS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HugAgentOS</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-28\">2026-09-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ZJU-REAL/HugAgentOS/tree/9436fcb77261bc995e6e7a45cdc5e192eae9bc74\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9436fcb77261bc995e6e7a45cdc5e192eae9bc74\">9436fcb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive project work through model-driven iterative reasoning, tool use and observation-driven continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous tasks, changing project/file state, tool/service results, browser/command outcomes, model uncertainty and evolving user context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-specific reasoning/tool actions and revise subsequent actions from returned observations until the bounded outcome is complete. - Decision owner: the model-driven ReAct actor assembled and executed through HugAgentOS&#39;s first-party runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: orchestration/workflow.py, core/llm/agentfactory.py, AgentScope integration, tool registry, sandbox/MCP execution, project/context/memory injection, streaming and approval controls. - Closure path: project objective + assembled context → model actor selects action/tool → HugAgentOS executes within the permitted boundary → result returns to the actor → later model action changes in response → project outcome advances. - Boundary reachability: ordinary first-party project-chat and supported background paths instantiate the ReAct runtime directly; no contributor-only actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor while retaining FastAPI, tools, persistence and sandbox leaves execution infrastructure but removes the task-specific discretionary action choice. - Evidence: document/en/modules/chat.md; src/backend/orchestration/workflow.py; src/backend/core/llm/agentfactory.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: AgentScope and model/provider internals remain dependencies. A credits the model actor reached through HugAgentOS&#39;s supported first-party harness path, not organizational functions internal to those dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a concrete runtime interference mode between concurrently dispatched S1-capable sub-agents by isolating each child&#39;s asynchronous execution/cancellation scope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple child agents using MCP/AnyIO machinery can otherwise create cross-task cancellation-scope errors that disrupt sibling operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine the execution-isolation boundary for each dispatched child so one sub-agent&#39;s async/cancellation lifecycle cannot corrupt another child&#39;s task scope. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party HugAgentOS runtime policy in callsubagent; no autonomous coordination actor chooses this isolation relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated ThreadPoolExecutor, one event loop per child thread, child cancellation bridge, isolated MCP clients and separated child runtime construction. - Closure path: parent dispatches distinct S1-capable children → unsafe cross-task async interference is separated by per-child thread/event-loop isolation → each child continues its own model/tool lifecycle → independent results return for subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: callsubagent is a shipped first-party tool and the isolation is its ordinary implementation at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the anti-interference policy remains if the model agents are replaced; deterministic runtime code decides and enforces the isolation boundary. The function is therefore constructor/runtime-owned C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: two or more concurrently dispatched model-driven child/main agents capable of substantive delegated project work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: cross-task AnyIO/MCP cancellation-scope interaction can raise runtime errors and interrupt sibling child execution when async lifecycles share an unsafe task boundary. - Attenuating coordination relation: each child executes in its own worker thread with its own event loop and isolated MCP/client lifecycle, keeping cancellation scopes inside the owning task. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: isolated children remain able to execute later model/tool steps and return results instead of failing from sibling cancellation-scope corruption. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping rests on an explicitly named cross-task failure mode and a first-party isolation mechanism intended to remove that interference. Delegation, routing and the pool are not independently treated as S2. - Evidence: src/backend/core/llm/subagenttool.py; document/en/modules/chat.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a narrow technical coordination function. It does not imply that all sub-agent routing, batching or delegation is S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-project current-control function is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: individual autonomous loops regulate requirement progress, retries, checkpoints, reviewer findings, stalls and hard limits; automation/batch paths regulate their own jobs. These are lower-scope execution controls rather than a distinct project-wide S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying whole-project current-control decision over active operational resources, commitments, priorities, constraints or synergy is established. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: autonomous-loop ledger, one-requirement-at-a-time driver, checkpoints, replan hooks, runaway breakers, background lifecycle, batch orchestration and user approvals. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planner/worker/reviewer agents make bounded task decisions while deterministic drivers enforce one loop/job&#39;s progression. Neither demonstrates a separate whole-project S3 owner. - Evidence: document/en/modules/autonomous-loop.md; src/backend/orchestration/autonomousloop.py; src/backend/orchestration/batchorchestrator.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: at a narrower recursion one autonomous-loop driver is a strong controller of its own worker/reviewer process; this assessment does not lift that local task controller into project-level S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently verify whether a worker&#39;s claimed autonomous-loop requirement is actually implemented by inspecting real project output through a separate read-only agent path, then return findings into correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker self-report can claim completion when underlying files/output do not satisfy the requirement; the ordinary worker path can therefore hide defects or absent deliverables. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect actual produced files/command evidence independently and return done, continue, offtrack or needhuman for the requirement. - Decision owner: the separate model-driven loop reviewer agent instantiated by the first-party autonomous-loop driver. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct reviewer context, read-only capability policy, same project sandbox, explicit acceptance criteria, structured verdict/evidence, independent second pass, sub-agent logs and deterministic driver routing. - Closure path: worker produces candidate + summary → driver starts independent reviewer with requirement/criteria but not worker conversation → reviewer reads/searches/runs read-only checks against actual project output → verdict/findings return to driver → non-done findings change subsequent work; accepted evidence permits ledger advancement. - Boundary reachability: autonomousloop.py invokes reviewrequirement in the shipped autonomous-loop path; docs expose the loop through the normal API/frontend and canrunautonomousloop capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the substantive audit judgment is produced by a distinct model-driven reviewer. Deterministic read-only restrictions and driver routing enforce the boundary but do not decide whether the output satisfies the requirement. - Claim being audited: the worker&#39;s claim that the current requirement is complete and its project artifacts satisfy the requirement/acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: worker summary and its ordinary model/tool trajectory, plus machine evidence produced during the work iteration. - Complementary access path: separate reviewer agent personally opens/searches the same project sandbox and may run read-only commands rather than trusting worker self-report. - Independence boundary: reviewer and worker use distinct contexts/agent executions; the reviewer does not reuse the worker conversation, is explicitly read-only and is deterministically spawned by the driver rather than by the worker being judged. - Who acts on findings: the autonomous-loop driver; continue/offtrack/needhuman feed correction/replanning/escalation, while only accepted evidence-backed completion advances the requirement ledger. - Evidence: document/en/modules/autonomous-loop.md; src/backend/orchestration/autonomousloop.py; src/backend/orchestration/subagents/loopreviewer.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ontology committee/review code is not needed for this finding; the credited path is the directly reachable autonomous-loop reviewer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: HugAgentOS preserves memory, observes tools/services and can adapt private capability from accumulated operational episodes, but the reviewed path does not maintain a distinct model of the outside/future environment and return adaptation options through a separate current/future organizational conversation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S4 prospective adaptation judgment/feedback right is established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project/user memory, automation/triggers, personal evolution, historical-episode mining, semantic SOP judge, private skill candidates and owner approval. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: personal evolution genuinely changes capability, but it mines the user&#39;s own historical operating episodes for repeated successful patterns. Internal learning from past operations is not enough by itself to establish Profile S4. - Evidence: src/backend/core/evolution/personal.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the negative finding distinguishes real history-driven capability adaptation from the specific outside-and-then organizational function required for S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain the durable project identity and ultimate operating rules that define project goals/scope, authoritative sources, work methods, output/quality expectations and operating boundaries for later project operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project drift, missing or obsolete durable rules, conflicting/incomplete operating instructions and changes to project goals, quality requirements or boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the authoritative root AGENTS.md content for the project and make that policy decision govern subsequent project-agent operation. - Decision owner: Base (A) mode — the model-driven project-init agent decides the exact rule update after inspecting current rules and representative project material, then persists and verifies it. Parent (P) mode — a legitimate project editor/admin directly edits the same authoritative root file through supported project-instruction surfaces. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProjectInstructionsService, project permissions, optimistic revision checks, project-bound instruction tools, runtime approval mode, context assembly and project system-prompt injection. - Closure path: identity/policy issue → project-init agent synthesis or legitimate parent edit → authoritative root AGENTS.md write → later project context reads changed instructions → project system prompt injects them → subsequent project-agent behavior is governed by the returned policy. - Boundary reachability: project instructions, /init, direct authorized instruction editing and automatic project-context injection are shipped Community Edition paths at the frozen revision. The documented auto and full approval presets provide supported write-permitted modes in which ordinary project-instruction persistence does not require a human to choose or approve the exact model-generated content. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in base mode, the operator selects the initialization operation but the model actor makes the semantic decision about which durable goals/workflows/quality/boundary rules to add or revise from project evidence. Removing the model leaves storage and permission machinery but not that project-policy judgment. In parent mode, the authorized human directly owns the authoritative policy edit. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what the project is for and the durable rules later project agents must follow, including goals/scope, important sources, workflows, output/quality expectations and operating boundaries. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Base (A) — the project-init model actor for the exact synthesized update in the autonomous initialization mode; Parent (P) — the authorized project editor/admin for direct authoritative edits. - Return-to-operation path: changed root AGENTS.md is the canonical project-instruction source, is loaded into project context, and is injected into subsequent project system prompts/conversations. - Evidence: document/en/modules/projects-myspace.md; src/backend/core/services/projectinstructions.py; src/backend/core/llm/tools/projectinstructionstool.py; src/backend/prompts/projectinit.md; src/backend/prompts/projectsection.py; src/backend/core/llm/agentfactory.py; document/en/modules/chat.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: /init is explicitly user-invoked and is instructed to preserve human-authored constraints. A credits the model-owned semantic project-policy decision within that supported mode; it does not imply authority to erase higher-level operator constraints or rewrite unrelated platform policy.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f53e2824a5e4f85955b21cb94f5d22f1ef4aad21/assessments/hugagentos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f53e2824a5e4f85955b21cb94f5d22f1ef4aad21\">f53e282 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T17:56:07Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/brenonaraujo/git-meta-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">git-meta-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/brenonaraujo/git-meta-harness/tree/167b13c66993fa0a5216e4be2fc9bf911cd02cf8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 167b13c66993fa0a5216e4be2fc9bf911cd02cf8\">167b13c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Independently implement or inspect product changes through distinct model-backed builder, architect and specialist personas, in contact with real issue requirements and repository work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar user requirements, changing source code, failing tool/build responses and task-specific choices about edits or investigations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Installed persona model acting via Hermes selects the content of source edits/technical output and returns a comment, PR, revised specification or real artifact to the project. - Decision owner: Project-specific model-backed worker personas materialized by gmh seed and launched through supported Hermes profile/runtime; external runtime supplies terminal/LLM execution but role-specific mission/contract is first-party. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persona Markdown, Hermes cron, spawn-persona.sh, issue/PR state, executable specialist commands, branch rules, local Git repository, deterministic CI/sensor gates. - Closure path: Project requirement → manager-dispatched persona issue/brief → worker chooses and executes terminal/source operations → comments/branch/PR/test outputs → returned GitHub state for subsequent work/next manager tick. - Boundary reachability: Source-owned seed.Apply writes persona and spawn contracts, loop installer schedules recurring manager launches, team-manager-tick.md calls spawn-persona.sh with a real named profile; this is an executable first-party installed operating path, not just a sample infographic. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The installed personas are explicit external-model-powered actors, and the content of implementation/technical judgments is not determined by a static issue-label script. Hermes owns tools, not the whole delivery governance. - Evidence: seed.go L1-145, spawn-tm.sh L1-24, spawn-persona.sh L1-23, team-manager.md L15-88, team-manager-tick.md L10-32. - Basis: Model-backed first-party materialized actor plus usable task/feedback path. - Confidence: Medium-high source-structural; live external-model session not run here. - Caveats: Merely executing gmh seed without Hermes gateway/provider/GitHub authorization does not produce completed S1 tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent destructive interference among independently running builder S1s that would otherwise edit overlapping source paths on parallel sub-issues. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simultaneous frontend/backend and same-layer builders can overwrite or break shared files/types and lose incompatible in-flight work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The materialized safety check compares actual path-scope/dependency metadata across candidate tasks, blocks uncontrolled overlaps and requires an explicit dependency/serialization or scope adjustment before dispatch. - Decision owner: The project actor/team manager and author of path-scope/dependency labels choose how to refactor/serialize; first-party sensor computes and enforces the specific interference check. No independent fully autonomous S2 agent decides compromise. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: path-scope: and depends-on: issue labels, check-parallel-builders.sh Python glob overlap analyzer, Sensor 10 and manager predispatch policy. - Closure path: Multiple ready/in-progress S1 builders → conflict in intended changed file scopes detected → fail/blocked state on missing/disputed scope → manager revises mutual scope/dependency or serializes → safe future builder launches. - Boundary reachability: Sensor 10&#39;s executable shell script calls GitHub to inspect actual issue labels and exits with blocking status for an uncovered overlap, while the installed manager&#39;s persona contract requires this check before issuing parallel work. Still requires the installed operator/manager to invoke and heed the gate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Detection is deterministic; the decisive policy/negotiation over which parallel work to delay is supplied by the project manager/label owners. Source provides a specific S2 constructor/enforcement path, not autonomous ownership. - Distinct S1 units: Independently spawned backend/frontend or multiple builder personas on separate live sub-issues/branches. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concrete concurrent edit overlap in paths/definitions (reported as root cause in the first-party Sensor 10 design note; not independently re-enacted). - Attenuating coordination relation: Block on non-empty path-scope intersection without declared depends-on, and force disjoint file scopes or explicit serial dependency before the manager starts workers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Failed executable check prevents uncontrolled concurrent in-progress launches; manager must change dependency/path assignments before resuming builder operations. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It detects a particular inter-builder source-file interference condition and specifically changes mutual scheduling; not simply following an issue DAG. - Evidence: check-parallel-builders.sh L1-210, 10-decomposition-safety.md L1-128, team-manager-tick.md L16-32. - Basis: Shipped context-dependent inter-S1 conflict detector and constructor routing; incomplete autonomous compromise ownership. - Confidence: Medium source-structural; glob overlap is heuristic and actual live gh queries were not reproduced. - Caveats: Missing gh or jq fails the check; manager must actually invoke the sensor in its installed mode. A comment-only division without enforceable predispatch use would not by itself establish S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Govern the current delivery project across ongoing independent specialist work cells by tracking the whole issue/PR state and making present assignments, commitment/intervention and closure decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting current priorities, stalled domain refinement, dead builders, unreviewed PRs, rejected QA, dependencies blocking delivery, excessive parallel workers and missing human validation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured model-backed team-manager reads open GitHub Issues/PRs at every cron tick, selects issue type/persona/branch, moves labels, limits worker concurrency, prompts failed/rejected work, defers blockers and determines when the project task is eligible for final delivery/human validation. - Decision owner: First-party materialized Hermes team-manager model-backed actor, separate from coding-worker personas and provided explicit team-wide current-delivery governance mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: gmh seed persona contracts, Hermes cron schedule, GitHub issue state, labels, branches, gh CLI, spawn-tm.sh, spawn-persona.sh, max-three-workers guard, QA and scope sensors. - Closure path: Whole current issue/PR/blocked/dependency/QA board → model manager chooses updated assignment/constraint/repair → changes GitHub label/branch or spawns named persona → subsequent specialist starts/stops/repairs work → manager sees next returned state and may adjust further. - Boundary reachability: loop.CronCommands creates recurring no-agent launcher of live model-manager plus orchestrator supervisor; installed scripts and materialized role source require actual GitHub state reads and actions, not merely a hand-designed static workflow graph. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A bare scheduler periodically launches the manager; the manager, not cron, exercises discretionary current staffing/recovery choices and holds the whole-board view. Removing that actor leaves no contextual policy owner for resolving delivery commitments. - Whole-system current view: gh issue list --state open, gh pr list --state open, issue labels/dependencies, QC/CI, waiting/failed workers and progress across the installed project&#39;s specialist cells. - Current-control decision scope: Decide if/which persona works next, maintain max three simultaneous workers, create shared branch, move commitments through states, block or repair stalled work, send QA rejection back to builders and gate closure. - Evidence: loop.go L103-164, spawn-tm.sh L1-24, team-manager-tick.md L1-32, team-manager.md L1-160, 07-hermes-loop.md L1-92. - Basis: Model-backed current whole-project regulator with evidence-returning resource and commitment effects. - Confidence: Medium-high source-structural, not a claim of user project&#39;s real deployment metrics. - Caveats: The authored role mandate alone is not sufficient; the shipped cron/launcher and tools close its reachability. Source does not prove the manager always obeys the prompt or that a real external Hermes gateway was run in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge builders&#39;/manager&#39;s ordinary claims of completed work with raw source/CI/tests/acceptance evidence and route failures to current managerial correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Builders may say pronto despite failing checks, security holes, missing contracts, visual regressions or incomplete DoD. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Distinct configured model-backed quality-assurance persona reviews the actual PR/DoD, tests/sensors and optional live snapshot, reports APROVADO versus REPROVADO and places the issue in qa or back in in-review. - Decision owner: QA model persona separate from implementing builder and issue-wide team-manager; GitHub CI/sensors provide independent observable facts, not autonomous audit judgment on their own. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QA persona, source PR diff, DoD issued by architect, static/security/contract/smoke/snapshot sensors, GitHub comments/labels, next manager cron tick. - Closure path: Builder PR/current completion claim → QA independently loads DoD and actual source/tests/CI → failed finding plus report/label in-review → model manager notices returned state and spawns builder for repair → new PR status/review. Pass returns qa, leading to human acceptance/merge control. - Boundary reachability: team-manager-tick.md explicitly spawns QA when PR+CI is ready and explicitly spawns a builder on rejected QA; quality-assurance.md defines executable commands, code/DoD inspection and label-producing return actions. Applies in the materialized first-party project flow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separately prompted QA specialist reads evidence rather than trusting the builder summary, forms an accept/reject judgment, and triggers corrective action through a distinct manager. Sensor exit codes are supporting evidence only. - Claim being audited: A coding persona&#39;s claimed implementation/PR readiness and fit to architecture-defined DoD. - Ordinary reporting path: Builder&#39;s branch/PR, completion comment, issue label in-progress/in-review and claimed tests. - Complementary access path: QA accesses raw branch/diff, code tests/security/contract/smoke checks and possible actual running snapshot independent of the implementer report. - Independence boundary: Distinct persona and process dispatched after builder, separate QA report; may share Hermes model vendor/infrastructure, so not vendor-independent. - Who acts on findings: Team-manager reassigns the builder on a rejected QA result, withholds merge pending green checks and/or human acceptance. - Evidence: quality-assurance.md L1-160, team-manager-tick.md L17-32, 02-pull-request.md L41-133. - Basis: Independent source/acceptance observation with active corrective return to current management. - Confidence: Medium-high conditional on configured Hermes and project test tools. - Caveats: Source does not demonstrate that listed external snapshot/Gatling/tool commands are installed in every adopter; source-specific QA decision and repair return, not every illustrative test, support the function.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Form future-oriented persona/skill improvement options from previously observed external project issue/comment evidence, with a first-party prescribed handoff but no self-contained completed autonomous application. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeated missing acceptance criteria, weak DoD, domain persona gaps, missing skills and changing project delivery needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic evolve.Propose selects issue-comment snippets by keyword and drafts suggested persona/skill patch prompts; a separate model-backed Hermes manager could choose actual changes and a human must explicitly validate before modifications. No in-product autonomous change/commit decision is made by this CLI. - Decision owner: CLI&#39;s keyword extraction and prospective proposal scaffold is first-party; decisive editing/adoption remains with external composed Hermes actor plus human approval. Hence C and not A, A(P) or P. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Issue/comment JSON fixtures, harness/memory/traces/&lt;utc&gt;, deterministic proposal.md, PROMPT.md, (--apply) only persistent trace write and explicit human validation instructions. - Closure path: Historical issue/PR comments → deterministic keyword/signal extraction → written prospective persona/skill adjustment proposal.md → human/Hermes handoff in PROMPT.md → possible approved persona/skill update → next project&#39;s persona execution. Return is documented as a required external composition, not executed within gmh evolve. - Boundary reachability: Shipped gmh evolve --from-dir ... --apply creates concrete trace and change prompt artifacts and existing harness/workflow/08-persona-evolve.md specifies who may act on them; however this command intentionally does not rewrite persona files and the model runtime/approval handoff is not natively executed, so no autonomous S4 closure is claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Keyword matching and prompt text generation are deterministic, not an agent choosing/accepting new future capability. The installed manager could respond to prompt only as separately composed external agent/user behaviour. - External distinction: GitHub issue/comment records reflect actual project/domain gaps and user acceptance problems from previous delivery, not an internal same-action memory reset. - Future / prospective distinction: Proposed edits target the persona/skill library for later issues, rather than simply retrying the current PR. - Adaptation option generated: Suggested changes to skill/persona instructions derived from historical AC/DoD/persona gap references, recorded for later human/editor decisions. - Path back into current capability / S3: PROMPT.md → external Hermes manager → required human validado → changes in harness/personas/ or harness/skills/ → future manager/builder ticks. This path is specified but not source-executed, a concrete constructor gap preventing A. - Evidence: evolve.go L1-187, evolve.go L1-92, 08-persona-evolve.md L45-112, EVOLVE docs. - Basis: First-party future-capability proposal and explicit constructible parent/agent-return path, not closed autonomous adoption. - Confidence: High on deterministic source limits, medium on hypothesized later handoff. - Caveats: --apply only writes traces and prompts. A README claim of evolving personas is not evidence they were changed; the human approval contract should not be counted as executed action.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No established legitimate ultimate organizational purpose/identity decision over the delivery organization is made and returned through this first-party harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requirements, project-specific persona names, GitHub merge protections and ordinary human acceptance can limit delivery but do not necessarily present a contested constitutional purpose/identity decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human specifies project requirements and may validate release/persona edits, while software enforces workflow stages; no first-party S5 actor legitimately resolves whole-system purpose conflicts and returns a revised ultimate policy. - Decision owner: The product adopter&#39;s true organizational leadership is outside the scoped generated delivery system, while team-manager&#39;s authority is current delivery S3 rather than supreme mission-setting S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: docs/SPEC.md, PROJECT.md, harness/AGENTS.md, GitHub PR protection, validado instruction and CLI persona domain policies. - Closure path: User spec / final acceptance → current delivery limits; missing legitimate identity/purpose conflict → terminal rightful decision → revised system-wide organizational identity applied to future operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Team manager routes development work; human PR approval and static constraints do not adjudicate legitimacy or constitutional identity simply because they can veto a merge. - Evidence: seed.go L1-145, team-manager.md L1-85, 08-persona-evolve.md L68-112, 02-pull-request.md L85-120. - Basis: Source-first bounded absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A customer organization may exercise real S5 elsewhere. Its existence does not flow into this product merely because someone provided a product spec.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e25bd40f14ddace94dea2df5d190b5a86b61edac/assessments/gi/git-meta-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e25bd40f14ddace94dea2df5d190b5a86b61edac\">e25bd40 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T03:39:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/samansarmasik-alt/forcecode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ForceCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/samansarmasik-alt/forcecode/tree/518672e170f570f59c2d2abdeb4b5cf148e1603b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 518672e170f570f59c2d2abdeb4b5cf148e1603b\">518672e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform uncertain coding tasks with model-selected repository inspection, editing, verification and repair actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing code requirements, project-specific tools, failed commands, missing artifacts and model/provider interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The active coding model or each independent read-only teammate chooses its next task-level tool action based on observed result. - Decision owner: First-party Agent model loop and child models, with deterministic tool/sandbox enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkspaceTools, ForceSandbox, model/provider interface, project artifact receipts, retry policy, process/session controls and task queue. - Closure path: Developer objective → AI tool/plan step → real file/shell/test result → observed evidence returned → next model decision or verified completion. - Boundary reachability: The shipped Force and forcecode.py entrypoint invokes the normal Agent; --team/VibeCode instantiate the same first-party model/tool operating loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses situational coding operations; static test gates cannot decide which change to make. - Evidence: Agent and managed-team run; execution/kernel; supported CLI. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate concrete concurrent-claim and shared-board state collisions between independently running worker terminals in the shipped terminal fleet. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two worker processes or actor threads could otherwise simultaneously read/update shared worker queues and reports, lose one another&#39;s updates or duplicate a claimed assignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Native exclusive file lock and atomic queue pop allow only one claimant/board mutation at a time; a worker receives only its claimed task and other terminals see updated state. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party queue/board primitives, not an autonomous actor choosing adaptive cooperation policy. The broader coordination response requires a composed manager/operator: C, not A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TerminalFleet.filelock, activetask/queue status, TeamBoard.lock and publish, shared report context and manager barrier. - Closure path: Concurrent attempts to mutate board or claim task → serialized lock/claim → one valid task assignment/update published → workers subsequently see distinct queued tasks/current reports rather than duplicate/lost state. - Boundary reachability: Persistent terminal fleet is an explicit built-in /terminal and worker mode; manager and workers share these first-party records with real worker claim/publish calls. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: File/queue locking and task claims apply fixed concurrency constraints; model discretion is for task assignment and report integration, not the lock arbitration. - Distinct S1 units: Separate model-backed worker terminals, each with its own read-only operative loop and queued assignments in persistent fleet mode. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent worker attempts to claim/edit shared queue/board state and overlapping assignment acquisition. - Attenuating coordination relation: Exclusive file lock during queue pop/report publish with one activetask per worker, plus synchronized manager phase/report board. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each terminal executes only its claimed task; next claim observes queue after prior mutation; manager sees updated reports for integration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The mechanism actively prevents lost-update and duplicate-claim interference across concurrent first-party workers rather than merely transporting messages. - Evidence: TerminalFleet.claim/publish and TeamBoard locks; fleet mode documentation. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Exercise whole-current managerial authority over live specialist workforce, assignment priorities, report integration and corrective action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple worker roles/queued jobs/results need coherent present commitments; integrated changes must be prioritized, verified or redone, and worker lifecycle may need adjustment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In autonomous managed-team mode the model selects distinct specialist assignments from the objective, observes shared reports at the barrier, then decides concrete integration edits and verification. In parent mode the operator sees fleet status and uses /terminal add/task/configure/remove to change current live commitments. - Decision owner: Root manager model for A mode; legitimate terminal operator for separate P mode. TeamBoard/TerminalFleet enforce state changes but do not make discretionary whole-team judgments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamBoard phase/reports, rundelegations supervisor, manager-only integration pass, TerminalFleet running roles/queues/states, dedicated /terminal commands. - Closure path: Manager views assignment plan and results → modifies worker commitments or integrates/fixes/validates current work → subsequent source state or worker tasks change; operator actions similarly mutate live fleet queues and active membership. - Boundary reachability: Built-in --team and /team managed mode and /terminal persistent fleet; model fleet action is explicitly user-authorized, and same first-party registry implements human command mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model chooses current workforce assignments and integrated corrective action in one legitimate mode; parent can independently exercise current whole-team control in a separate supported mode, so A(P), not automatic S3 in solo turns. - Whole-system current view: Shared TeamBoard assignments/reports/phase plus TerminalFleet activetask, pending queues, current worker PID/status/report records. - Current-control decision scope: Allocation of specialist responsibilities and current worker resources, termination/reassignment, multi-worker integration and verification of collective outcome. - Evidence: runmanagedteam, TeamBoard, TerminalFleet; headless/interactive managed team modes; mission read-state boundary. - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: medium-high for code-reachable mode; effective multi-agent performance not experimentally verified - Caveats: Ordinary single-agent ForceFlow remains S1-level work. MissionView only projects work state; it is not independently credited as S3.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Obtain independently sampled operational artifact evidence and a distinct reviewer judgment to challenge the completed VibeCode task outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The implementing agent may report success despite placeholder code, broken UX, missing tests, unsafe integration or package/build defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate read-only model reviewer examines actual project files and selects acceptance/failure findings; parsing enforces output shape, while the executing VibeCode loop returns its gaps as repair work. - Decision owner: Independent read-only reviewer Agent in first-party VibeCode completion mode; main coding agent acts on findings but does not author the audit verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runvibecodereview separate Agent with readonly=True/no history persistence, deterministic project-wide gate, typed pass/score/gaps format, parsevibecodereview and ForceFlow repair queue. - Closure path: Coding/verification receipts → separate reviewer instructed to inspect important files for missing implementation/integration/test evidence → independent structured gaps/pass → if failed append repair items and re-run implementation/quality loop; if accepted complete and persist report. - Boundary reachability: Shipped /vibe and /mission long paths invoke built-in runvibecodereview as final acceptance stage of the actual VibeCode loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer model generates source-informed judgment; deterministic JSON parsing and workqueue enforcement do not decide which defects exist. - Claim being audited: Main agent&#39;s completion/acceptance claim for the objective and implementation status. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementer tool/test receipts, task queue status, changed-file list and deterministic local gate. - Complementary access path: Separate read-only Agent with its own context directly inspects project files against the objective and catches defects not established by normal receipts. - Independence boundary: Reviewer is a new readonly=True Agent, recordhistory=False and no permission to edit; it receives objective/test/task evidence but must independently read actual project artifacts. - Who acts on findings: VibeCode current-control execution consumes gaps into repair queue, then root model implements fixes and repeats final review. - Evidence: runvibecodereview and VibeCode repair loop; VibeCode documented mode; receipt and repair gate. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party closed external-and-prospective organizational intelligence loop distinct from current coding/verification and manual provider setup is proven. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New provider capabilities, different project needs and tool ecosystem changes may matter; configurable providers and model routing do not show independently selected future adaptation programs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator chooses provider/model and package configuration; active coding agent can research the present task, not demonstrably decide future organizational capability in an S4→S3 relationship. - Decision owner: Human for config/product update, coder for current task; no distinct S4 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model selection, provider profiles, graph/repo intelligence, web lookup, skill loading, mission/vibe planning and snapshots. - Closure path: Config or task feedback → current model/operations; no outside-horizon scan → adaptation option → authority decision → returned changed team capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Current task planning, self-repair and model selection do not count as future/environment S4 without the required prospective coupling. - Evidence: provider model and Agent loop; configuration; model and extension controls. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime ultimate organizational purpose/constitutional policy adjudication loop with a returned whole-system mandate is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Safety sandbox, modellock, tool approval and read-only workers govern execution privilege, not the organization&#39;s ultimate identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator approves potentially risky actions and chooses provider/model, while deterministic sandbox enforces bounds; no distinct S5 identity owner. - Decision owner: Human for local trust and model choices, fixed runtime policy for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ForceSandbox, Smart Autopilot, modellock, worker role privilege, app config, redaction and workspace safety controls. - Closure path: Selected permission/model policy → restricted/approved tool; missing ultimate-policy issue → legitimate S5 decision → binding returned organization identity mandate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Enforcing a local safety rule or choosing a product role is not constitutive identity governance. - Evidence: sandbox; agent/fleet permission constraints; project security policy. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cfd322a4f8fe8eca7ab1b2275fed55ab7a28cd28/assessments/forcecode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cfd322a4f8fe8eca7ab1b2275fed55ab7a28cd28\">cfd322a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T20:02:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/W-Mai/atman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Atman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/W-Mai/atman/tree/56933fc40e38ccb5575cbfdda341d86a449643ac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 56933fc40e38ccb5575cbfdda341d86a449643ac\">56933fc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed code work within programmed .at agent flow, with tool/result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown files, failures in shell/test/edit commands, incomplete tasks and differing child work outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose native tools and code edits or next model goal in response to prior tool results, within typed flow permissions. - Decision owner: first-party managed Atman model-directed coding actor operating through llm.call and dispatchall; the .at script defines bounds rather than individual tool choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: managed AGENTAT, flow executor, llm.call, tool dispatch, session messages, native Git/fs/shell tools. - Closure path: user task→llm.call→model-selected tools→dispatchall executes→session.push feedback→subsequent model/tool choice or finished response. - Boundary reachability: atman init installs native managed flow; CLI/TUI default route and atman run can execute it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses concrete coding operations and adapts to observed tool results; full .at control-flow ownership is deterministic but does not eliminate adaptive S1. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/templates.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/executor.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/llmcall.rs, README.md. - Basis: first-party supported native model loop and tool feedback. - Confidence: high for source-reachable operating path, no live task outcome test. - Caveats: CLI/DSL custom flows without model-choice tools can be deterministic rather than S1=A; current assessment is for shipped managed agent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: construct isolation that attenuates concrete shared-file write interference among separate coding child S1 actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent child flows writing overlapping files in the same parent checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: allocate different Git worktrees for each child when workspace:auto/retain is requested, rejecting mismatched repository bindings and preserving separate result paths. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic workspace constructor (C) under operator/parent requested workspace policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: managed worktree service, per-child workspace id/lease, repository-root and owner validation, propagated child execution cwd and lifecycle finalization. - Closure path: parallel child spawn with workspace policy→FlowWorkspaceService.allocate creates child-specific Git worktree→child fs/bash/git tools use isolated working directory→distinct result files and lifecycle return avoid concurrent writes to parent&#39;s checkout. - Boundary reachability: native flow.spawn accepts workspace auto/retain in installed runtime, default none remains non-isolated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic first-party isolation changes the contested writable work domain, but model arbitration of semantic patch conflicts is not demonstrated. - Distinct S1 units: separate first-party subagent.at implementer or other child coding flows spawned from a parent session. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent editing of identical files or commands in the same repository checkout by multiple independent children. - Attenuating coordination relation: unique managed per-child worktrees and guarded workspace ownership isolate each writable Git checkout. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: accepted children execute in distinct roots; invalid bindings fail instead of permitting conflicting edits to parent root; child outputs return with workspace scope. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: explicit per-child writable workspace isolation attenuates inter-worker resource contention, not just sequencing or status transport. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/flowworkspace.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/gitworkspace.rs, README.md. - Basis: supported optional first-party collision attenuator. - Confidence: high for static worktree allocation, no concurrent live trial. - Caveats: worktree policy is opt-in; it does not resolve merge conflicts across resulting patches.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model-owned whole-current management of ongoing child coding and verification flows in managed agent mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate tasks, stalled or irrelevant child work, tasks needing reassignment, overall progress and running/background child status. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect whole visible session&#39;s child population and decide which to spawn, reuse, kill, steer/interject or await, changing real execution commitments. - Decision owner: first-party managed primary model invoking flow.instances/status/output/spawn/kill/interject as separate child-control tools; registry owns safe execution of its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session-scoped FlowInstances live inventory and one-use spawn token, model tool choices, native child registry, lifecycle/status and output, async watchers. - Closure path: current worker inventory and task outputs→primary model interprets whole group→kills duplicate work, spawns new work with token or redirects active child→tool execution mutates live child state→updated inventory/results feed back to parent. - Boundary reachability: templates.rs ships a managed agent with these exact exposed and recommended tools; first-party flow.instances and control actions are available when group exists, not hypothetical user-authored software. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model makes conditional whole-team current decisions from a live group view; unlike a fixed DSL stage list this changes commitments to address current work state. The same model can do S1 code work, but the child portfolio decision right is a differentiated metasystem function in the supported delegation mode. - Whole-system current view: session-scope inventory of all visible spawned child handles, goal, status, model, run id, output and watchers, plus parent goal and prior task results. - Current-control decision scope: choose child assignment and active/cancelled commitments, reuse current work, redirect or terminate a worker rather than merely issue own file actions. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/templates.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/flowlist.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/agentctrl.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/taskregistry.rs. - Basis: built-in autonomous primary supervisor over subordinate first-party flows. - Confidence: medium-high for supported path, dynamic decisions not live witnessed. - Caveats: without spawned children, the ordinary agent loop does not expose a whole-team portfolio to regulate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge candidate completion using a distinct model-backed disposition judge and, in explicit verification mode, an independently tasked read/test child whose findings can drive correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: premature final.answer, candidate summaries omitting required work, unsupported completion claims and untested code modifications. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge completion relative to original request and recent transcript, trigger continueaction/continuework instead of returning; in verify-child mode separately select file/read/test evidence actions and report independent findings. - Decision owner: first-party separate llm.classify model invocation at managed agent handback, and optional first-party verify-role model actor on distinct child flow; parent receives findings for corrective coding. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTAT managed final-answer disposition schema, recent-turn evidence, answer origin, injected continuation directives, first-party SUBAGENTAT verifyloop with restricted tools and separate session. - Closure path: producer candidate final response→separate llm.classify checks request/transcript and emits disposition→negative continuation gate appends correction to live parent agent; optional verify child reads/tests repo directly→findings returned via flow output→parent implementation/decision. - Boundary reachability: installed atman init managed agent automatically calls disposition judge for no-tool final candidates; dedicated verify-role subagent is shipped and model-callable but optional. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a distinct model invocation/child role, not the producer&#39;s self-claim, chooses whether reported work is sufficient; the first-party flow enforces re-entry. It does not automatically verify all code with a test suite. - Claim being audited: user-request completion for a proposed final answer; optionally correctness of implementation as observed by dedicated verify child. - Ordinary reporting path: producer model response with final.answer, tool transcript and running child work. - Complementary access path: classifier gets independent memory.recentturns evidence and original request, separate from candidate text; optional verify child can read source/Git diff and run tests in its own model/tool loop. - Independence boundary: completion classifier is a separate model call with a role-specific category contract, not a producer statement; verify role is a separate first-party child run with read/test rights. Neither implies externally attested reviewer independence. - Who acts on findings: managed flow directly blocks premature exit by injecting a continuation directive, and parent agent consumes child verification findings and may repair work before a later answer. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/templates.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/llmclassify.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/finalanswer.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/tools/agentctrl.rs. - Basis: source-confirmed separate model judgment and binding narrow completion return; optional read/test evidence mode. - Confidence: medium-high for configured first-party pathways. - Caveats: classifier&#39;s evidence is a recent transcript excerpt, not an independent workspace check; stronger code-claim audit depends on explicitly spawning verify role. Absence of an observed live model decision is not hidden.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external/future intelligence actor selecting and rolling out durable new capabilities from environmental evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task failures, stored confessions, rule changes and changing context are addressed through ordinary memory and coding control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: detect outside/future change and decide a prospective installed capability adaptation with binding return into new operations; not established. - Decision owner: developer/operator can edit .at flows or memory manually; no first-party prospective strategy-selection organ demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory.confess, rules, history, project goals, flow authoring and tool availability. - Closure path: durable confessions inject corrective hints into current/future coding, but do not autonomously choose new tools/flows or govern future systemic capacity under a distinct owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: remembering errors and allowing DSL editing are useful but not a genuine outside-and-then option→implementation cycle. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/memory/confession.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/templates.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/flowregistry.rs. - Basis: bounded negative first-party source inspection. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: user can construct S4 using external flow programs, which are not the credited standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate identity/purpose interpretation organ with independent legitimate authority beyond user-defined task and runtime security policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: untrusted flows, unsupported permissions, scope changes and spec design uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate governing system identity and ultimate organizational purpose with binding decisions for S3/S4; not established. - Decision owner: legitimate user authorizes specific spec designs, sandbox capabilities and commands; DSL enforces these bounds but does not independently decide organizational constitutional policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static flow contract.scope checking, capability sandbox, human form.ask with revision-bound approval receipts, TUI permissions and role prompts. - Closure path: spec confirmation grants/denies exact design, tool permission allows or denies a call; neither establishes a rightful ultimate identity owner governing system ends. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: narrow task approvals and exact-revision authorization are operating governance, not distinct S5 identity/purpose self-rule. - Evidence: crates/atman-runtime/src/approval.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/permission.rs, crates/atman-runtime/src/templates.rs. - Basis: bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an adopting organization can supply external S5 policy and custom flows, outside this published harness&#39;s recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ec3da6b24a3c0f24211b112c75b66db92476a90a/assessments/atman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ec3da6b24a3c0f24211b112c75b66db92476a90a\">ec3da6b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T12:20:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/chankov/agent-fleet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent Fleet</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/chankov/agent-fleet/tree/d260099e67911d38f7cfbe90bd30b2639c2fd4b6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d260099e67911d38f7cfbe90bd30b2639c2fd4b6\">d260099 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: actual coding, testing, research and artifact delivery by selected model-backed specialists or the hub operator agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task requirements, source errors, tool failures and repository test feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: specialist model chooses read/edit/test/research steps and integrates feedback to complete assigned work. - Decision owner: model-backed Pi specialist (or directly operating hub model), instantiated with first-party persona and tool scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party persona definitions, spawnPiAgent composition, dispatch returns, bounded subprocess/runs, repository tools. - Closure path: hub dispatch → spawned coding/research persona → source/test interaction → structured result and artifact path → hub follow-up or user outcome. - Boundary reachability: agent-hub is wired into just fleet and selected team personas are installed; real model/registry and Git workspace are external prerequisites. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model executor leaves a typed dispatcher and state, not substantive problem solving. - Evidence: .pi/harnesses/agent-hub/README.md; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; .pi/harnesses/agent-hub/dispatch-native-complete.ts; agents/builder.md; package.json - Basis: explicit first-party orchestrated mode and code paths -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: narrow interference attenuation when multiple independently started agent-driven flow integrations would write the same repository. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous conflicting modifications of the same worktree and failure to preserve one writer at a time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: allow or refuse acquisition of one live writer lease for the effectful poll merge, returning holder identity to the competing flow. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party writer-lease constructor; the independent model voices/integrator own their own work, not the lease logic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: atomic exclusive lock creation, PID liveness/reaping and finally-release; poll apply gating. - Closure path: two effectful polling/merge flows target one cwd → first lease holder proceeds → live contender receives WriterLeaseHeldError → later flow can retry after the prior release. - Boundary reachability: withWriterLease is invoked by the shipped effectful poll merge and is a concrete narrow first-party coordination path, not hypothetical general mailbox semantics. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2-specific conflict resolution is deterministic; no model autonomously assigns global worktree exclusion across the whole hub. - Evidence: .pi/agent-fleet/scripts/workflows/lib/writer-lease.ts; .pi/agent-fleet/scripts/workflows/lib/merge.ts; .pi/agent-fleet/scripts/workflows/wf-poll.ts; docs/workflows.md - Basis: implementation + documented opt-in flow mode - Confidence: medium; scoped to effectful flow merges - Caveats: hub&#39;s concurrent writable specialist scope violations are only advisory; the lease is not a universal lock. - Distinct S1 units: separate model-backed poll integrator runs/independently initiated effectful flow cells in a shared repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two competing model-led integration attempts can overwrite or interleave changes in one worktree. - Attenuating coordination relation: atomic exclusive writer lease based on repository path with PID-aware contention refusal. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the refused run does not apply its write, while a holder completes/releases before another may proceed. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: exclusion explicitly regulates actual conflicting effectful sibling S1 write rights, not just their messages or scheduled order.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current control of the configured multi-specialist task as one local fleet, including priority, delegation, verification follow-ups and resource allocation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unbounded specialist spawning, incomplete assertions, failed outputs, context pressure and mismatched task progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: hub model selects assignments, next intervention, corrective dispatch, pause or request for human assistance from aggregated work status. - Decision owner: Pi-backed hub model guided by first-party orchestration prompt/commands; hard tool budgets and watchdog only enforce constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tier limits, roster, model routing, assertion ledger, dispatcher results, zoom/status dashboards, askuser and budget guard. - Closure path: request/task ledger and specialist result → hub current view and model judgment → dispatch/plan/retry/clarification → new specialist action and evidence/status. - Boundary reachability: explicit configured orchestrator with roster gives the hub active dispatch tools, result/history/ledger and decisional prompt; not claimed for bare empty-roster default. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without model hub judgment a gate can refuse spawns but cannot choose the current fleet goal, re-plan work or reassign specialist attention. - Evidence: .pi/harnesses/agent-hub/README.md; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; .pi/harnesses/agent-hub/dispatch-native-complete.ts; .pi/harnesses/agent-hub/assertion-ledger.js - Basis: explicit and structural model-control mode - Confidence: medium-high for conditional standard mode - Caveats: generic askuser approval is not separately credited as a closed parent S3 mode; no claim of hard guarantees for model prompt compliance. - Whole-system current view: current task/role roster, outstanding verification assertions, active child states, structured returns, time/dispatch budgets and human blockers. - Current-control decision scope: choose which specialist executes which present commitment; retry, stop, re-scope, verify or escalate work based on returns.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: conditional separate code-review judgment on implemented work, feeding concrete findings back to the task-owning hub. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation claims or assertions that look complete but diverge from changed source, tests, architecture or security constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: read-only code-reviewer model independently inspects repository changes and issues findings/risks rather than copying builder status. - Decision owner: distinct reviewer persona&#39;s model; hub dispatcher handles corrective assignment and receipt of findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: code-reviewer read/bash/grep/find/ls tools; preflight/quality/perf/doc reviewer subroles; direct diff and project rules; structured artifacts, reviews path. - Closure path: builder artifact/diff → separately dispatched reviewer with direct source/rules → quality findings → hub receives evidence and can issue builder correction/re-review. - Boundary reachability: first-party installed code-reviewer is callable through native dispatch when roster contains it; a separate full review is conditional, not default on every task. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent reviewer makes a separate model judgment, while the dispatcher and deterministic evidence/scope mechanisms do not themselves acquire audit autonomy. - Evidence: agents/code-reviewer.md; .pi/harnesses/agent-hub/README.md; .pi/harnesses/agent-hub/dispatch-native-complete.ts; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: shipped persona and dispatched operating mode - Confidence: medium: independent access is structural, not an observed review outcome - Caveats: this is not a universal mandatory merge gate; review not invoked in bare default mode and no institutional outsider independence is claimed. - Claim being audited: producer&#39;s completed implementation and evidence meet requirements, project rules and quality/security expectations. - Ordinary reporting path: builder status, structured success output and self-reported assertions. - Complementary access path: reviewer reads actual Git change set, files, project rules, tests and optional independent subreviewer findings. - Independence boundary: separate read-only code-reviewer specialist and independent review role versus producing builder; both remain within the same configured hub. - Who acts on findings: hub model delegates rework or asks the human; no hard automatic block of every task is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: conditionally turn observed user/environment corrections or repeated task failures into durable rules affecting future agent behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future tasks differ from stale practices; repeated user correction, failed test assumptions and drift in project conventions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimate operator/human accepts or rejects a specific proposed lesson diff before rule/document changes that later sessions load. - Decision owner: human parent in the explicitly invoked compound-learning mode, not the session&#39;s documenter model or deterministic diff writer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party af-compound command, compound-learning skill, documenter persona, evidence collection, target rules/docs index and diff verification. - Closure path: past user corrections/test failures → model finds candidate future rule → human approves relevant diff → write/dedupe checked → next task loads updated rules/docs and changes work choices. - Boundary reachability: af-compound is a shipped command invoking the model documenter; compound-learning specifies approval or explicit standing authorization and a verified write to declared rules/docs targets. Requires an opted-in user and project rules surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the option is generated by a model, but the effectful long-term adaptation choice belongs to the human; without human authorization (or preauthorization) no learning diff is applied. - Evidence: .pi/harnesses/agent-hub/commands/compound.ts; skills/compound-learning/SKILL.md; agents/documenter.md; .pi/harnesses/agent-hub/README.md - Basis: first-party skill and model/human operational contract - Confidence: medium; prospective path source-documented, no executed multi-session learning trial - Caveats: only the explicit compound-learning mode is credited, not generic memory, simple rechecking tests or all future changes; published P remains a source-based hypothesis for Index S1 adjudication. - External distinction: feedback from human requestor/corrections and actual tests/repository conventions rather than mere current task status. - Future / prospective distinction: curated durable prompts/rules guide later independent tasks and sessions, not only repairing the present broken test. - Adaptation option generated: a precise new/modified repeatable operational rule with evidence, source and deduped target. - Path back into current capability / S3: operator-approved rule is written to indexed rules/docs and read by future role/hub work, altering next operational decisions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct ultimate identity/ethos policy settlement for the whole fleet is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: basic mission and normative tensions between current work and future adjustment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinct legitimate ultimate-policy judgment plus return into operating fleet would be required; no such first-party closed loop established. - Decision owner: humans select policy and approve operations/compound diffs; these are task/quality/future-rule choices, not independently established whole-system S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: work modes, persona config, skill policies, damage controls, approval prompts, role labels. - Closure path: configuration changes constraints and local work, but no distinct whole-fleet identity conflict → ultimate authority → policy settlement → binding return was identified. - Boundary reachability: inspected shipped team-policy, approval, user and adaptation paths; the bounded absence is about first-party local fleet, not an operator organization&#39;s possible S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the presence of human yes/no and safety defaults does not itself identify an S5 owner or organizational policy closure. - Evidence: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; .pi/harnesses/agent-hub/README.md; .pi/damage-control-rules.yaml; skills/compound-learning/SKILL.md - Basis: bounded negative scope, no assumption of universal impossibility -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/48b3d4638e3f312c7a61ca690897b58d7a4b1721/assessments/agent-fleet-chankov.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 48b3d4638e3f312c7a61ca690897b58d7a4b1721\">48b3d46 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-28T19:40:58Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/matt82198/aesop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aesop</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-28\">2026-09-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/matt82198/aesop/tree/2a661c12007c5484f964176da3d5532c6bdf070d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2a661c12007c5484f964176da3d5532c6bdf070d\">2a661c1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive software-development work on bounded project backlog items through model-driven coding workers that inspect/edit repository state, run tools/tests and return implemented changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous implementation tasks, changing code and dependency state, ambiguous task requirements, test/build failures, tool/model uncertainty and task-specific acceptance criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-specific implementation/tool actions, interpret repository/test observations and revise subsequent coding actions until a bounded work item is complete or fails. - Decision owner: the dispatched model-driven worker reached through Aesop&#39;s first-party worker/harness path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentDriver, work orders, owned-file boundaries, workdirs/worktrees, test commands, verification tiers, repair caps, durable journals and backend adapters. - Closure path: selected backlog item + work order → model-driven worker chooses implementation/tool actions → repository/test observations return → later worker actions change from those observations → structured/filesystem result returns to the Aesop wave. - Boundary reachability: /buildsystem directly dispatches model-driven coding workers, and the backend-portable AgentDriver/wavebridge path is shipped first-party runtime at the frozen revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven worker while keeping scheduler, filesystem isolation, gates and persistence leaves coordination/control infrastructure but removes the semantic implementation decisions that produce the software outcome. - Evidence: README.md; skills/buildsystem/SKILL.md; driver/agentdriver.py; driver/wavebridge.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider/model internals remain dependencies. A credits the model-driven worker actor reached through Aesop&#39;s shipped orchestration boundary, not autonomy of deterministic driver/scheduler code.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate destructive interference among concurrently active coding-worker S1 units by refusing overlapping file ownership before dispatch and isolating admitted workers in independent worktrees/branches. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two workers modifying the same project files or mutable working tree concurrently can overwrite, race with or contaminate one another&#39;s changes and create unstable integration state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine which current items may coexist without ownership overlap and enforce separated mutable workspaces for the workers that are allowed to run in parallel. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party scheduler/preflight/worktree machinery. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: normalized ownsFiles, disjoint-item selection, overlap abort/skip, git worktrees/feature branches and serial merge integration. - Closure path: candidate concurrent items expose owned-file sets → deterministic preflight rejects/defers overlaps → admitted workers receive separate workspaces → each worker&#39;s later coding/tool actions proceed without sibling intermediate edits → completed branches/results return for controlled integration. - Boundary reachability: the shipped /buildsystem contract requires disjoint ownership/worktree isolation, while driver/wavescheduler.py mechanically selects file-disjoint items in the executable backend-portable wave path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the inter-worker attenuation decision is encoded and enforced by deterministic first-party rules. Model workers remain autonomous inside their local tasks but do not decide whether ownership overlap is permissible. - Distinct S1 units: two or more concurrently dispatched model-driven coding workers operating on separate backlog items. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent writes to overlapping project files or the same mutable working tree can collide or contaminate sibling work. - Attenuating coordination relation: reject/defer overlapping ownsFiles before dispatch and isolate admitted worker writes in separate worktrees/branches. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflicting workers are not jointly admitted; admitted workers continue later implementation/tool actions only inside their isolated ownership/worktree boundary, and their results are integrated after local work completes. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mapping rests on an explicit concurrent-write conflict and mechanisms specifically intended to prevent that cross-worker disturbance, not on the mere presence of a fleet, task queue or dispatcher. - Evidence: skills/buildsystem/SKILL.md; driver/wavescheduler.py; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the MCP read-only status surface explicitly does not implement real-time coordination; it is not used for this S2 claim. The credited coordination path is ownership/workspace interference attenuation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current project wave as a whole by prioritizing active backlog commitments, assigning bounded work to operational units, monitoring current fleet/test/liveness state and deciding retry, triage, review/integration and wave-closure actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing backlog priorities, dependencies, file ownership conflicts, stalled/failed workers, failing tests/PRs, budget/liveness constraints and the risk that locally successful worker changes are not suitable for the current project wave as a whole. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current project priorities/assignments and exception responses across the active fleet, including which items to dispatch, how to triage/retry failures and which current results advance through review/integration. - Decision owner: the model-driven main-thread orchestrator executing the shipped /buildsystem procedure in the credited mode. - Whole-system current view: project-root CLAUDE.md, current backlog/STATE/BUILDLOG, dispatch assignments, worker heartbeats/status, build/test/PR results, cost and current wave progress are assembled for the orchestrator across the active project wave. - Current-control decision scope: rank priorities/dependencies, assign workers, react to stalls/failures, choose redispatch/manual-triage paths, regulate review/integration and close/checkpoint the current wave. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic wavescheduler selection, HALT/cost gates, file-ownership guards, tracker/journal state, verification policy, repair caps, watchdog/liveness checks and merge/checkpoint machinery. - Closure path: current project backlog/state + fleet signals/results → model orchestrator evaluates project-wide priorities/exceptions → dispatch/retry/triage/integration choice → first-party machinery enforces/records that choice → changed tracker/repository/wave state governs subsequent worker dispatch and wave actions. - Boundary reachability: /buildsystem is a shipped Aesop skill intended for the ordinary installed Claude Code harness path; the README/setup exposes installation/use of these skills, and the frozen skill explicitly executes the rank → dispatch → monitor → review → checkpoint wave. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic scheduler/gate code can still enforce overlap, cost, lifecycle and tracker constraints, but it does not make the same semantic project-wide priority/triage judgments with the orchestrator removed. In the credited /buildsystem mode those current-control choices are made by the model-driven orchestrator. - Evidence: skills/buildsystem/SKILL.md; docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; driver/wavescheduler.py; driver/orchestratordriver.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the backend-portable Python scheduler also exposes substantial constructor-owned current control. Methodology 0.3.x has no A+C composite; A records the ordinary supported model-orchestrated /buildsystem mode in which the decisive whole-wave semantic choices are agent-owned, while deterministic machinery is recorded separately as support/enforcement.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently challenge an already-green worker result by replaying its exact verification command on the orchestrator side and revoke green status when the independently observed result disagrees. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker execution or self-report can produce a fake-green condition in which an item appears verified even though the claimed test does not actually pass when independently repeated outside the worker&#39;s ordinary result path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: re-run the exact testCmd against a deterministic sample of already-verified items and invalidate the item when the replay exits non-zero. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party Phase 5.5 exact-gate runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic sampling by slug, AgentDriver.runcommand, separate gate exit capture, fakegreen marker, durable journal update and downstream verified gating. - Closure path: worker result is already verified=True → orchestrator-side exact-gate independently re-runs testCmd → non-zero replay flips verified=False and records fakegreen → later review/ship logic sees the revoked status and does not treat the item as green. - Boundary reachability: verifyexactgate is invoked directly in the shipped runwave phase sequence after repair and before adversarial/final-catch/ship handling; the active verification policy gives every supported tier a positive spot-check fraction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment here is the independently observed command exit and deterministic comparison, not a model&#39;s semantic reviewer discretion. Therefore ownership is constructor/runtime C even though model-driven workers produced the audited changes. - Claim being audited: the operational worker/wave claim that the current item is genuinely verified/green according to its declared test command. - Ordinary reporting path: the worker/driver build path and its initial test/verification result used to set verified=True. - Complementary access path: a later orchestrator-side replay independently executes the exact verification command for a deterministic sample rather than trusting the prior worker/result status. - Independence boundary: replay occurs after the ordinary build/repair path through separate orchestrator-side command execution; its verdict depends on the new process exit result, not on worker narrative or cached success. - Who acts on findings: waveloop; replay failure revokes verified, marks fakegreen, persists the audit result and thereby changes downstream shipping/review eligibility. - Evidence: driver/waveloop.py; driver/agentdriver.py; driver/verificationpolicy.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Aesop also contains model adversarial-review and orchestrator final-catch surfaces. They are not required for this mapping: at the frozen revision, Python adversarial-review activation/configuration is less cleanly aligned with narrative defaults, while final-catch itself notes limited evidence richness. The exact-gate replay supplies the narrower closed S3 witness.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material distinct external-and-prospective adaptation function is established inside the assessed project/wave organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Aesop records incidents, audits completed waves, probes backend capability, compares architectures/models and can feed retrospective findings into a later backlog, but the reviewed standard project runtime does not maintain a separate model of the relevant outside/future environment and return prospective adaptation options through a distinct S3↔S4 conversation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying project-recursion external/future adaptation judgment/feedback right is established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: closing audit, incident history, durable STATE/BUILDLOG/MEMORY, monitor signals, backend capability probes, verification-tier policy, benchmarks/A-B studies and next-backlog notes. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retrospective audit and internal operational learning can change later work, and backend probes can adapt current verification strictness, but those are internal history/current execution regulation unless they form a distinct prospective environmental-intelligence function. The reviewed project runtime does not establish that separate S4 organization. - Evidence: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; driver/agentdriver.py; driver/verificationpolicy.py; docs/INCIDENTS.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the negative finding is recursion-specific. Aesop&#39;s repository-development organization may conduct substantial R&amp;D/benchmarking, but adjacent maintainer/R&amp;D activity does not become S4 of an ordinary deployed target-project wave merely by repository co-location.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain the durable project identity and ultimate operating rules that define project purpose, domain boundaries/contracts, build/run expectations, gotchas and standing orchestration constraints for later Aesop-managed work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing or stale project identity, inconsistent worker assumptions, conflicting domain contracts, drift in standing development/orchestration rules and loss of durable project intent across sessions/restarts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the authoritative project/domain CLAUDE.md content that later /power, /buildsystem orchestration and workers must load/follow. - Decision owner: Base (A) mode — the model-driven /power init-prime orchestrator synthesizes the exact project/domain rule layer from repository evidence and writes/pushes it. Parent (P) mode — the legitimate project owner/editor reviews, edits or iterates those same authoritative files directly. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only exploration fan-out, repository scans, git persistence/push, secret-scan gate, /power fast-path loading, /buildsystem prerequisite/rule loading, work-order constraints and scoped domain files. - Closure path: project identity/policy gap or owner change → model init-prime synthesis or legitimate parent edit → authoritative project/domain CLAUDE.md is persisted → later /power loads/internalizes the changed rules → subsequent /buildsystem orchestration and worker work orders operate under that durable layer. - Boundary reachability: /power is a shipped first-party skill; README/setup installs Aesop skills for ordinary use, and the frozen skill explicitly implements both autonomous init-prime writing and already-primed rule loading. Direct owner iteration of project/domain CLAUDE.md is part of the documented supported project workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in base mode the user invokes /power, but the model actor inspects repository evidence and chooses the exact synthesized purpose/gotcha/domain-contract content rather than merely storing preselected text. Removing the model leaves file-writing machinery but not the semantic project-policy synthesis. In parent mode the legitimate owner directly chooses the authoritative rule edit. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what the project is for and which durable contracts/rules govern later Aesop work at that project recursion, including purpose, domain boundaries, build/run commands, verified gotchas and standing orchestration expectations. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Base (A) — model-driven init-prime orchestrator for the synthesized project rule layer within higher-level Aesop cardinal constraints; Parent (P) — legitimate project owner/editor for direct authoritative project-rule changes. - Return-to-operation path: project/domain CLAUDE.md files persist in the target repository; later /power explicitly loads them as authoritative semantic memory/rules and /buildsystem requires that loaded rule layer before dispatching/controlling subsequent workers. - Evidence: skills/power/SKILL.md; skills/buildsystem/SKILL.md; tools/initproject.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: autonomous project-policy synthesis remains bounded by higher-recursion Aesop/global cardinal rules and by the repository facts the init-prime path is instructed to preserve. A(P) does not claim that the model can rewrite those higher-level constraints; it records two supported ownership modes for project-recursion policy.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fe62356055b27197969e5b8c762ab168c54f361e/assessments/aesop.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fe62356055b27197969e5b8c762ab168c54f361e\">fe62356 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T03:19:38+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CorticalComputer/Spartan\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Spartan</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CorticalComputer/Spartan/tree/8f7b36a2326c0157a82040f8e04b474f4899c3da\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8f7b36a2326c0157a82040f8e04b474f4899c3da\">8f7b36a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Persistent autonomous local entity operating loop: select model-authored task actions, change files/console/memory and continue from returned tool observations without a new human prompt. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Novel machine contents, external user or peer observations, tool errors, incomplete objectives, backend failures and bounded context history. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Concrete configured LLM model chooses JSON actiontype/arguments every cognitive cycle; first-party dispatchactions calls executeconsole, view, writefile, blockreplace, updateworkingmemory and other handlers; observations feed next provider call. Initiative can invoke new cognition without user input. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed Spartan entity owns agent task decisions. Deterministic Python dispatcher executes tool choices; human controls host/config/credentials but not each action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ResilientProvider, continuous cognitiveloop, converthistorytomessages, TOOLMAP, dispatchactions, persistent session, FileWatcher and self-alerts. - Closure path: External observation or autonomous initiative → provider action objects → dispatch tools → action and result recorded to conversation history/state → subsequent prompt reincludes observations → new agent decision; process can persist/recover. - Boundary reachability: The normal GUI and headless shipped paths both connect supported real providers to cognitiveloop and TOOLMAP and expose proactive takeinitiative; the model can act on actual new tool outputs and not only a static mock plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Counterfactually deleting the model removes task-contingent choice while leaving the Python dispatcher and watchdog; discretionary action is agent-owned. - Evidence: source; source; source; source - Basis: First-party explicit code and supported runtime modes; no empirical provider invocation in this assessment. - Confidence: High for S1 decision closure at one agent recursion. - Caveats: Unsandboxed shell/filesystem access is deliberately unsafe; no reliability, security or deployment-performance claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced inter-S1 conflict-damping relation among continuing viable autonomous entities; peer links transport messages but do not regulate collective variety. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Spawned Spartan entities may send alerts, have competing objectives, overload contacts or fail independently. - Decisive decision or feedback right: SpartanRadio whitelist and FileWatcher deliver alert messages; SpartanLink shows status to a human. No autonomously authoritative interentity commitment balancing or conflict resolution is defined. - Decision owner: Individual entity models, deterministic inbox code, human SpartanLink operator; none assigned S2 mutual-regulation decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Radio file alerts, allowed sender list, rate limiting, spawn/terminate scripts and Link roster. - Closure path: Message → target inbox/context → target entity discretionary actions; no inter-unit disturbance diagnosis → balancing S2 decision → corrected mutual operations. - Boundary reachability: Agents may be independently viable, but their communication is not shown to attenuate clashes or oscillations across their goals. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: All shipped routes are reachable as optional peer modes, but none closes S2. - Evidence: source; source; source; source; source - Basis: Bounded negative from direct first-party messaging/fleet review. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Private real-world fleets are not credited as source evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separate whole-current discretionary manager of several autonomous entities&#39; resources, priorities, commitments and accountability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Child process failure, resource/budget shortage, changes to single entity&#39;s task focus and competing peer demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An entity edits its own Working Memory and Grand Strategy and can spawn or terminate drones. Deterministic watchdog restarts or rolls back crashes; this is not a binding, organization-wide manager with a whole portfolio view. - Decision owner: Single entity controls its own work, watchdog/launch scripts enforce host lifecycle; collaborator may operate Link but does not receive a supported standing S3 parent mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WM/GS, spawndrone, terminatedrone, SpartanLink status UI, resilience/watchdog. - Closure path: Per-entity observation → local task update; local crash → restart/rollback. No whole-system current-state report → accountable allocation decision → return changing different viable S1 units. - Boundary reachability: Single-agent goal management is not an independently authorized current management layer; optional drone creation does not automatically grant their resource/priority authority. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Optional scripts and peer interfaces were inspected; none provides a separately functioning S3 executive. - Evidence: source; source; source; source; source - Basis: Bounded negative after checking strongest fleet and human-parent paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A separately governed deployment could supply such an owner beyond the pinned distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of S3 ordinary management claims with distinct direct-reality access and returned corrective S3 decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Hallucinated model assertions, incorrect tools, bad persistence, crashed process, mistaken peer status. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Mindfulness second-pass critiques the same model&#39;s draft. Watchdog verifies process health. Neither is an independent reality auditor over an S3 manager. - Decision owner: Same Spartan model and deterministic lifecycle monitors, not separately mandated auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional two-pass mindfulness, history observations, LTM, watchdog and human UI. - Closure path: Model draft → same model correction → next own task answer, or watchdog crash → restart. No S3 report → independent field finding → S3 correction. - Boundary reachability: Self-critique does not satisfy independence and task observation does not establish organizational management audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Mindfulness and peer status are first-party but do not implement the audit relationship. - Evidence: source; source; source; source - Basis: Bounded negative after checking all available audit-like surfaces. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Do not deny value of task checking or infer an external reviewer without source.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Outside-and-then future intelligence over the entity&#39;s prospective objectives and operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New external file/peer/tool information, changing research goals, memories/ideas about opportunities and threats, future provider or tool limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent model may on its own examine observations, choose time-horizon GrandStrategy objectives, issue updategrandstrategy, then use the changed plan to guide WorkingMemory and new tools. Agent can also switch backend or edit scripts for capability adjustment. - Decision owner: Persistent autonomous entity owns future-goal judgment; developer Genesis provides framing and runtime carries out writes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Continuous initiative scan, first-party file/console observation, long-term semantic recall, horizon-labeled Genesis strategy contract, GS update tool, L4 prompt assembly. - Closure path: Outside input/recall → model selects prospective strategic revision → updategrandstrategy persists state → next assemblelayer4 presents revised GS → model reworks current WM and actions. - Boundary reachability: The current normal cognitive loop actually uses model-selected GS edits and then rebuilds next-cycle context with that GS; no external human must accept the entity&#39;s future objective. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The actor chooses what future goal to pursue; fixed state writer is merely a mechanism, and the returned goal can affect subsequent action. - Evidence: source; source; source; source - Basis: Source-backed prospective option and returned operating feedback at the single-entity recursion. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Unmeasured plan quality; not a claim of company-level foresight or effective market intelligence. - External distinction: FileWatcher, tools and LTM expose changing host/peer/external observations, while initiative mode can prompt scanning for actionable objectives. - Future / prospective distinction: Genesis explicitly distinguishes weeks/months/years/decades horizons and separates Grand Strategy from immediate Working Memory. - Adaptation option generated: Agent-selected updategrandstrategy may introduce or replace future-oriented objectives, and other supported tools can switch backend or change technical files. - Path back into current capability / S3: Updated GrandStrategy is reinserted into Layer 4 every next cognitive step and drives current WM/tool work. No independent S3 is claimed; the feedback returns directly to current S1.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A - Function: Single-entity foundational identity and ultimate internal policy: self-authored Charter of Self directs the entity&#39;s purpose, constraints and subsequent strategy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New substantial experiences or identity conflicts after continuous operation/backend changes can challenge standing self-principles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent model can choose addcharterentry with a selected MANDATE, CONSTRAINT, PRINCIPLE, DIRECTIVE or other charter entry and rationale; first-party code stages then flushes it to Soul/CharterOfSelf.md, which is fully included in later system prompts. - Decision owner: At this local entity recursion, the agent owns self-charter amendment decisions within developer&#39;s fixed Genesis Core/host envelope. Human host has physical sovereignty but no separately closed first-party parent-S5 decision loop is credited. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: addcharterentry in TOOLMAP, staged knowledge buffer, Sleep Cycle flush, Charter file unlimited load into assemblesystemprompt, Genesis identity→strategy→tactics hierarchy. - Closure path: Self-deliberated policy issue → model-authored charter amendment → staged and flushed to disk → complete Charter reappears in future system prompt → subsequent strategic and operative decisions use self-authored constitutional rules. - Boundary reachability: Charter edit is a supported real agent action path, not documentation alone, and the stored entry returns as an authoritative identity layer in a future normal provider call. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent selects and authors enduring policy rather than merely executing a human configured toggle; fixed Genesis and host control bound this sovereignty to its own agent recursion. - Evidence: source; source; source - Basis: Explicit local identity ownership and return path, not a metaphysical/organizational-personhood claim. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Genesis is developer-defined and immutable by normal prompt contract; amendments are additive and need successful flush, and host can ultimately stop/edit the system. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whether a new durable principle, mandate, constraint or directive should change the single entity&#39;s own enduring purpose, rights, obligations or identity. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: In autonomous entity mode the model authors its own Charter amendments. This is not legal sovereignty or authority over host machine and no parent S5 is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: addcharterentry → staging buffer → Sleep Cycle flush → Soul/CharterOfSelf.md → full Charter loaded on next system prompt → identity-guided GS/WM/current tool choices.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/326b42b8c8251dc6da508b0db7037df640aef8fb/assessments/sp/spartan.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 326b42b8c8251dc6da508b0db7037df640aef8fb\">326b42b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T03:17:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/joshuasetiawann/relaycli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RelayCLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/joshuasetiawann/relaycli/tree/2102796a136f996cbc48402cfb00304f5614e03e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2102796a136f996cbc48402cfb00304f5614e03e\">2102796 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously produce software-engineering outcomes by inspecting a project, editing files, running commands/tests and completing focused implementation goals. - Disturbance / variety regulated: codebase structure, implementation choices, test/build failures, tool errors, model/provider variability, local task ambiguity and project-specific constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive implementation/investigation actions and determine how to respond to tool/model evidence within the assigned goal. - Decision owner: each active model-backed RelayCLI coding Agent, including scheduled specialist agents in the parallel mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolRegistry/ToolContext, permissions, project-root confinement, model routing/escalation, call/token budgets, session trimming, file cache and path leases. - Closure path: user/parent task → coding Agent interprets goal and project evidence → model selects tool/edit/test actions → tool results return into the Agent loop → Agent revises work until it completes or stops. - Boundary reachability: the Agent loop and coding tools are the shipped runtime used by the default parallel task agents and the fallback single-agent/sequential relay modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed Agent leaves permissions, limits and tool executors but no open-ended choice of implementation or corrective action. - Evidence: relaycli/agent/loop.py; relaycli/agent/orchestrator.py; relaycli/core/roles.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every spawned process/task label is automatically an S1; the positive claim is limited to model-backed operational agents that own focused software-engineering outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent destructive interference among concurrent coding S1 units by assigning explicit work/dependency/path-claim boundaries and enforcing mutually safe execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the concrete collision mode in which two concurrent agents edit overlapping paths/files and can corrupt each other&#39;s work, plus dependency-order interference among tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide each task&#39;s role, dependency edges and anticipated path claims, thereby defining which operational units may safely proceed independently and which interactions require ordering/serialization. - Decision owner: the model-backed Orchestrator agent that emits the task graph; it chooses dependson and pathclaims from the user request before the scheduler executes them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskGraph readiness, deterministic Scheduler concurrency cap, LeaseManager overlap detection/acquisition/release, write-tool lease checks and task failure/block propagation. - Closure path: Orchestrator emits task/dependency/path-claim constraints → Scheduler compares graph readiness and active leases → conflicting units wait or unauthorized writes fail → lease/dependency state changes after completion/failure → affected S1 units become runnable/blocked accordingly. - Boundary reachability: parallel orchestration is enabled by default at the frozen revision; cli.py dispatches ordinary one-shot requests into runparallelwithview, which constructs the Orchestrator, graph, Scheduler and LeaseManager. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime deterministically enforces reservations, but removing the Orchestrator leaves no discretionary first-party actor deciding the task partition, dependencies or path claims from the request. The scheduler does not invent those organizational constraints itself. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent role-specific coding Agents created by TaskAgentFactory for graph tasks whose goals can independently produce project changes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping file/path edits can corrupt work; the lease module explicitly names this collision, and graph dependencies encode ordering needed when units are not independent. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-selected dependency/path-claim boundaries plus scheduler serialization of overlapping claims and per-write lease enforcement. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a lease conflict keeps an otherwise graph-ready task from launching; completion releases the lease so it can proceed, while failed dependencies block descendants and lease violations fail the attempted write. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path-lease mechanism is explicitly built around a structurally evidenced cross-agent collision, and the model-selected claims/dependencies directly determine mutual adjustment between peer coding units rather than merely transporting messages. - Evidence: relaycli/agent/orchestrator.py; relaycli/agent/graph.py; relaycli/agent/scheduler.py; relaycli/agent/leases.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: arbitration after the Orchestrator&#39;s graph decision is deterministic. The A claim is for agent ownership of the discretionary coordination constraints, not for the LeaseManager itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: provide whole-run current control over active commitments by exposing current lanes/graph state to a parent user who can intervene in one task or stop the run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: wedged or failed tasks, commitments that should be dropped, newly relevant instructions for a running lane, blocked descendants/leases and a run that should be stopped. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether a current task is cancelled, retried, steered with a new instruction, or whether all current work is stopped. - Decision owner: the human operator in the supported interactive live-lane mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live frame/status/lane rendering, Scheduler requestcancel/requestretry/requeststeer, graph reset/block logic, lease release, Agent steer inbox and stop polling. - Closure path: parent observes current whole-run lane state → selects drop/retry/steer/stop → UI dispatches the decision to Scheduler/Agent → graph/lease state or the agent&#39;s next iteration changes → subsequent current operation reflects the intervention. - Boundary reachability: the live lane control surface is wired into ordinary parallel execution when the run is interactive, terminal-capable and in full-auto mode; unsupported display conditions fall back to progress lines rather than silently claiming the same parent-control surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the one-shot Orchestrator creates the initial task graph but is not re-entered with live Scheduler outcomes, and deterministic scheduling/failure propagation does not own a discretionary whole-system current-control judgment. The closed intervention mode belongs to the user. - Whole-system current view: the live frame reads the Scheduler&#39;s current graph, task statuses, leases, outcomes/activity and selected lane while the run is active. - Current-control decision scope: parent intervention over active commitments via drop/cancel, retry (including unblocking descendants), steer of a selected live agent and stop-all. - Evidence: relaycli/ui/live.py; relaycli/agent/scheduler.py; relaycli/agent/loop.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: static graph decomposition, concurrency limits, budget stops and automatic subtree blocking are not credited as autonomous S3. The parent-control path is mode-specific rather than guaranteed for every invocation.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect implemented work against the request/plan and return an approve/revise judgment that can force another coding cycle. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect or incomplete implementation claims, divergence from the requested plan, failing verification and defects not visible in the Coder&#39;s ordinary self-report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the actual working tree, run verification as needed, and issue the review judgment VERDICT: approve or VERDICT: revise. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed Reviewer Agent in the supported sequential relay mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read/exec-only reviewer capability boundary, verdict parser, bounded review-cycle counter and deterministic Relay loop that forwards revise feedback to the Coder. - Closure path: Coder completes and reports → Reviewer receives request/plan/report but also inspects actual working tree/tests independently → Reviewer issues verdict → revise is returned to the Coder as corrective feedback or approve terminates the quality loop → subsequent operation changes. - Boundary reachability: sequential relay is shipped and reachable by disabling the on-by-default parallel path and enabling relay (for example --no-experimental-parallel --relay); the Reviewer role and loop are first-party runtime code, not a repository-development-only evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Reviewer itself makes the substantive audit judgment; parsing VERDICT and looping are deterministic support. Its direct working-tree/test access is materially different from trusting the Coder&#39;s report. - Claim being audited: the Coder&#39;s claim that the current implementation satisfies the user&#39;s request and the Planner&#39;s plan. - Ordinary reporting path: the Coder&#39;s own final text/report and normal implementation tool results. - Complementary access path: a separate read/exec Reviewer Agent is instructed to inspect the actual working tree and can run tests before issuing a verdict. - Independence boundary: the Reviewer is a separately instantiated model-backed agent with no write capability in its role, separate prompt/model routing and direct evidence access; it is not the Coder rephrasing its own completion statement. - Who acts on findings: the deterministic Relay loop routes a revise judgment back to the Coder for another bounded cycle; an approve judgment closes the run. - Evidence: relaycli/relay.py; relaycli/core/roles.py; relaycli/cli.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the frozen CLI checks experimentalparallel before relayenabled, so relay review is not the default path and --relay alone does not bypass an otherwise enabled parallel mode; the positive claim is limited to the reachable sequential-relay configuration.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying S4 disturbance is placed under a loop that senses changing external/future conditions, develops adaptation options and returns them into present capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable markdown memory, the remember tool, researcher/web tools, model-provider health tracking/escalation, skills and persistent configuration can inform later work but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an Agent may save a fact because future sessions may need it and later sessions receive memory in their system prompt, but this is memory update/reuse rather than an evidenced external-and-prospective option-development conversation with current S3. Provider escalation reacts to current call failure rather than modeling future adaptation. - Evidence: relaycli/core/memory.py; relaycli/tools/remember.py; relaycli/agent/router.py; relaycli/core/roles.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: task-local web/research can gather external facts, but no standard first-party loop was found that turns those facts into prospective adaptation options for the harness and returns them to current capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established at the run recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level conflict or S3–S4 policy tension is shown reaching an ultimate authority and returning as governing policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes, security prompt text, project-root/secret protections, role capability definitions, persisted model/relay settings and user approvals constrain operations but do not create identity-policy closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither the Orchestrator nor operational agents have authority to redefine RelayCLI&#39;s identity or ultimate policy, and routine user permissions/settings are operational constraints rather than S5 decisions. - Evidence: relaycli/core/permissions.py; relaycli/core/config.py; relaycli/core/roles.py; relaycli/agent/loop.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a human can change settings and approve or stop ordinary actions, but no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy issue and return-to-operation governance path is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/72b0d86107735bb21dfffc3a98e59087cfba122c/assessments/relaycli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 72b0d86107735bb21dfffc3a98e59087cfba122c\">72b0d86 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T03:49:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aa-blinov/cast\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cast</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aa-blinov/cast/tree/8eb5142ed05b2d835c4da64487b12aff4712c6c7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8eb5142ed05b2d835c4da64487b12aff4712c6c7\">8eb5142 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce and revise project-facing implementation/research actions through an open-ended model/tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, task ambiguity, external model responses, tool errors, tests, permissions, context limitations, changed requirements, and child findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose next repository tool/action and change course after observing its real result. - Decision owner: active model-backed cast coding persona, or each model-backed mutating child within its delegated task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapter; model-specific tool schemas; first-party read/write/edit/bash/search; permissions; plan/build mode; session store; tool hooks; context/memory; child task transport. - Closure path: user/project context → model selects tool/subtask → first-party dispatch/permission path → code or environment result → result reenters the model&#39;s later choices, or the model completes. - Boundary reachability: the shipped CLI/headless/web entrypoints instantiate the ordinary loop; coder-with-subagents is shipped and enables the model-visible task path in supported runtime modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor, tool definitions and deterministic gates cannot choose arbitrary task-specific edits and iterative debugging steps. - Evidence: src/core/loop.ts; src/core/tools.ts; src/core/tools/task.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is code-level evidence for shipped behavior; no live provider-backed agent or tool mutation was run in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: dampen a concrete conflict between separately running child S1s contending for the same interactive bash approval input. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent subagent confirmation prompts could race for the shared terminal/stdin and apply the wrong approval or interleave prompts, disturbing child work. - Distinct S1 units: separate task children each execute their own model/tool loop and may request bash confirmation while active concurrently. - Inter-S1 disturbance: the task runtime explicitly documents confirmation races between concurrently running subagents sharing one terminal. - Attenuating coordination relation: serializeConfirm chains their shared-terminal confirmation calls, forcing each to finish before the next begins. Per-session acquireSlot additionally caps child model streams, but slot count alone is not the reason for S2 credit. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: after its serialized approval or rejection, each child receives its own permission result and can continue or revise its next action; a failed/aborted child result propagates to parent tools. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the serializer is expressly keyed to actual cross-child contention over one approval input, not mere message passing or fan-out. Its effect is on tool authorization for the independent children. - Decisive decision or feedback right: establish which simultaneous child&#39;s approval interaction may occupy the shared input at a time, preserving correct response routing. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party confirmation serializer orders the requests; no model-decided conflict-specific reprioritization or autonomous coordination owner for that channel is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: promise-chain serialization, child tool confirmation callbacks, bounded slots and actor lifecycles. The human still owns individual allow/deny choices where permissions ask; those are operational authorizations, not autonomous S2 arbitration. - Closure path: competing child bash confirmations → per-process serialized confirmation queue → one prompt and response at a time → correct child&#39;s model/tool loop observes approval/rejection → later child action reflects that outcome. - Boundary reachability: the task tool passes serializeConfirm to its child runAgentLoop directly in the shipped CLI/interactive execution path; no external plugin is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: remove the parent model but keep active children and serializer: FIFO interference attenuation still occurs. The exposed S2-specific path is real, but agent-owned coordination discretion is not closed; this is a constructor/support-level C, not A. - Evidence: src/core/tools/task.ts; src/core/loop.ts. - Basis: structural + explicit code comment. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: child file-write scopes are advisory prompts, not first-party workspace collision arbitration; no broader inter-agent semantic coordination is inferred. In unattended modes without interactive confirmations the cited S2 path may be dormant.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: supervise and intervene in the current bounded population of subordinate model-backed work units from the local operator control room. - Disturbance / variety regulated: out-of-date or unproductive active child commitments, conflicting immediate priorities, and unwanted background agents consuming current effort. - Whole-system current view: the web server lists all child sessions of the selected parent session, with task ID, subagent/persona, model and running status; the operator can inspect their separate session histories. This is whole-current only for the declared one-session organization, not a multi-project company control plane. - Current-control decision scope: the operator can choose an identified running child to stop; the server checks that the child belongs to that parent and calls cancelTask, which aborts the live actor. Supported web controls also permit changing which project/session work is attended next. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which current subordinate commitments should remain active or be terminated based on the parent session&#39;s live child list. - Decision owner: the human operator of the built-in local web mode (P). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listSubagentSessions; runningTaskIds; cancelTask; actor registry and abort signals; authenticated web endpoint/session UI; durable session records. - Closure path: current child population/status visible through /api/sessions/:id/agents → operator chooses a child to cancel → /api/sessions/:id/agents/:taskId/cancel → actual actor receives cancellation → later population/status and work change. - Boundary reachability: both child-list and cancel endpoints are implemented in the shipped cast web server and call the first-party session and actor state. No independent operator application is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model-facing task can delegate and steer child IDs, but lacks an equivalent model-visible whole-population list/cancel path; delegation alone is not sufficient S3. Removing the web operator eliminates the established whole-current intervention decision without changing the lower-level deterministic actor registry. - Evidence: src/server/server.ts; src/server/bridge.ts; src/core/tools/task.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is local attended supervision. It does not support a claim of autonomous S3 or a cross-workspace whole-organization manager.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the implementing actor&#39;s correctness, security, test coverage and design claims using a separately instantiated review child. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overlooked regressions, missing tests, insecure shell/code changes and implementer self-confirmation. - Claim being audited: the parent coding result is adequately correct and safe to finish or present. - Ordinary reporting path: the implementation parent&#39;s own tool output and completion narrative, plus same-loop /review machinery where explicitly requested. - Complementary access path: the model-visible task chooses subagent=&quot;review&quot;; execTask loads the shipped separate reviewer system prompt, starts a separate model/session/context with read/grep/glob/ls/bash inspection tools, and returns its own file/line findings as a tool result. - Independence boundary: the reviewer has isolated context and cannot see the parent&#39;s reasoning; it inspects direct repo files. The reviewer role omits write, edit and recursive task. It may use the same model/provider and its inspection-only bash restriction is partly prompt-enforced, not a separate sandbox; these limit strength, not the independent judgment channel. - Who acts on findings: parent receives the review report as an ordinary task tool result and can revise code/tests, request a follow-up, or challenge completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently judge defects and severity from direct repo evidence rather than accept implementer summary. - Decision owner: model-backed built-in review subagent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-specific prompt, tool allowlist, execTask session/actor isolation, extractTaskResult, child-result taskBlock, parent tool loop. - Closure path: implementation performed → parent model invokes reviewer with project scope → isolated read/search/Git review → reviewer returns severity/location findings → parent model receives the findings and can correct/retest/re-review. - Boundary reachability: coder-with-subagents is an included persona with subagents: true; review is a shipped subagent prompt and appears in the standard task schema, which is enforced both on advertisement and execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the reviewer model leaves only isolation and tool plumbing, not an autonomous substantive code-defect judgment. - Evidence: prompts/subagents/review.md; prompts/personas/coder-with-subagents.md; src/core/tools/task.ts; src/core/loop.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: selecting the ordinary qa persona or calling /review in the same active loop alone is not this positive path; the separately spawned review child is.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: detect repeated patterns in the changing project/task environment and package prospective reusable workflow capabilities for later sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring user-project needs, repeated costly operations, stale memories and loss of prior project practice when sessions change. - External distinction: incoming real user/project trajectory across previous sessions, project memory, and existing project assets are compared rather than only the current model turn; the model sees repeated environmental demand for a workflow. - Future / prospective distinction: the distillation prompt explicitly selects workflows worth applying in future work, ordinarily requiring recurrence rather than a one-off task. - Adaptation option generated: model chooses a bounded reusable skill or persona, or declines to create one when evidence is weak. - Path back into current capability / S3: first-party materialization writes .cast/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md or .cast/personas/&lt;name&gt;.md; trusted-project skill/persona discovery loads these for subsequent coding sessions, expanding the available operating repertoire. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge which historical environment patterns merit adoption as future runtime skills/personas and author the reusable capability. - Decision owner: model-backed first-party maintenance/distillation run. Deterministic parsing, repeated-evidence checks, file validation and project leases constrain its adoption. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /distill; optional memoryDistillAuto; scheduled maintenance actor; trajectory/memory retrieval; hasRepeatedWorkflowEvidence; materialization and later skill/persona discovery; project-trust gate. - Closure path: multiple sessions&#39; project/environment evidence → model-based distillation selects future workflow capability → first-party writer materializes trusted project skill/persona → subsequent runtime reload/discovery exposes it to later models → operating capability changes. - Boundary reachability: this is packaged in src/core/memory.ts, wired to the first-party /distill command and opt-in automatic maintenance at new sessions; it does not borrow a separate research harness or marketplace plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the maintenance model, deterministic interval scheduling and file checks cannot decide which reusable workflow to invent or distill from varied project trajectories. - Evidence: src/core/memory.ts; src/ui/commands.ts; src/core/settings.ts; src/core/personas.ts; src/core/skills.ts; prompts/memory-distill.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: memoryDistillAuto defaults off and opt-in/manual activation is required; the prospective horizon is future local project workflows, not autonomous market reconnaissance. Source wiring supports the claim but the quality of generated adaptations was not experimentally tested here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime ultimate identity/policy decision-and-return loop is established for the assessed one-project organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: trust, tool permissions, personas and user-authored rules constrain actions, but an identity/ultimate-policy conflict is not adjudicated through a legitimate internal or parent S5 circuit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established at S5 level; user/developer-authored settings supply fixed limits or ordinary tool approval. - Decision owner: not established in the assessed operating boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project trust; permissions; persona allowlists; read-only plan mode; custom skills/rules; system prompts and user configuration. - Closure path: configuration and permissions are loaded/enforced, but no first-party ultimate-policy issue → legitimate decision owner → authoritative resolution → system-wide governed return was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic operator approval, trust toggling and changing a persona do not create identity-level legitimate authority or an S5 closure path. - Evidence: src/core/permissions.ts; src/core/personas.ts; src/core/plan.ts; src/core/settings.ts. - Basis: explicit configuration + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: repository maintainer and user ultimate-policy authority exist externally, but their governance is not a closed first-party runtime identity circuit at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/173ff97e738da8f56c6ad071fd1d79dcff4ec866/assessments/ca/cast.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 173ff97e738da8f56c6ad071fd1d79dcff4ec866\">173ff97 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T16:44:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/black141312/ada\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ada</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/black141312/ada/tree/20c3dd8813ce82fc82c2b243094ea239502e029f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 20c3dd8813ce82fc82c2b243094ea239502e029f\">20c3dd8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform requested project inspection, coding, editing and related tool-driven work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar projects, user goals, files and tool failures, integration results and changing local context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each Agent model selects tools/arguments and its next response after seeing prior observations. - Decision owner: primary or child model-backed Agent operating within an assigned task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, session history, execution wrappers, permissions, token limits, project settings and model-client adapters. - Closure path: request → model chooses action → tool executes → feedback enters the session → model revises the next action or completes. - Boundary reachability: both terminal and ada serve construct the shipped Agent; registered native tools and separately spawned child Agent sessions are reachable in the supported runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model decisions vary with tool feedback; the deterministic tool layer carries out selections but does not preselect each task action. - Evidence: src/client/agent.ts, src/client/cli.ts, src/client/background.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural; first-party deterministic script selfcheck of individual mechanics. - Confidence: high for shipped model/tool ownership. - Caveats: no live LLM agent execution or outcome-effectiveness measurement was performed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: damp conflicting writes by separate delegated operating agents in one parent-owned worktree task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two independently running child agents may both modify the same repository path, producing destructive overwrite or inconsistent integration. - Distinct S1 units: separate delegated Agent subprocesses, each selecting and executing its own work in a detached git worktree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping changed paths when two workers independently edit the same tracked or untracked file. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent Agent.claimPaths reserves each changed path; runIsolatedWorker copies only unclaimed outputs into the parent&#39;s tree and reports rejected collisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the parent receives [not applied — another worker already owns: ...], so integration/next delegation can take the conflict into account; overlapping child outputs are not both applied. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the path detects an actual inter-agent file collision, atomically reserves affected output paths and prevents competing writes, rather than only launching or sequencing children. - Decisive decision or feedback right: deterministic first-claim reservation/denial for an S2-specific conflict path; the parent receives the result but no autonomous negotiation/resolution policy is established. - Decision owner: constructed parent-level collision circuit, not a proven independent adaptive S2 agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated git worktrees, git status --porcelain -uall, per-parent claimedPaths set, copy-back filter and collision text. - Closure path: distinct worker edits → changed-path comparison → parent claim/deny → only allowed edits applied → collision returned into parent work/next delegation. - Boundary reachability: spawnSub calls runIsolatedWorker in a git repository by default; the claim callback is wired to the parent agent and changes real copy-back behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the conflict-damping function is concrete, but its rule is deterministic. A model can respond after observing a collision, not autonomously choose or revise the coordination rule itself; hence constructor C, not A. - Evidence: src/client/worker.ts, src/client/agent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the isolated-worktree path. - Caveats: collision reporting does not negotiate a merged edit; in-process fallback, non-git projects and other independent Ada processes lack this same file-reservation coverage.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: observe and intervene in the current running agent population, including cross-run steering and operator cancellation of currently assigned work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mismatched live priorities, stuck or misdirected concurrent work, current job progress and commitment to still-running tasks. - Whole-system current view: in one runtime process, listagents enumerates all registered live runs with task, age and context size, with peekagent providing recent output/usage; in attended CLI/editor mode, /jobs and GET /v1/jobs enumerate the current background-job cohort and progress. - Current-control decision scope: an autonomous Ada run can select a peer and send context, priority corrections or stop requests to its next-step mailbox; an attended operator can select and cancel a currently running background job via the exposed first-party control surface. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in autonomous mode, the model determines which registered run needs intervention and its steering content; in parent mode, the human decides which background task to abort. - Decision owner: agent model for the bounded live-run mode; human operator for the bounded job-management mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: process-local runs map and steer queues, registerRun/endRun, listagents/peekagent/sendagentmessage, jobs.json job roster, AbortController, /jobs and REST cancel route. - Closure path: live peer information → agent management decision → message enters peer steer queue → peer processes it at its next step; or background status → operator cancel decision → abort controller changes job execution and persisted status. - Boundary reachability: src/client/live.ts registers shipped tools used by agents and Agent.send() creates live run IDs; src/client/cli.ts supports /jobs and GET /v1/jobs/POST /v1/jobs/:id/cancel for actual background jobs started by shipped backgroundtask. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the autonomous model selects an intervention using live portfolio information, beyond ordinary own-task execution; the operator branch has separate legitimate human decision rights and operational return. Neither UI nor abort mechanism alone would establish S3. - Evidence: src/client/live.ts, src/client/background.ts, src/client/agent.ts, src/client/cli.ts, src/selfcheck.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural, with offline stub-run tests for live roster/messaging and job cancellation. - Confidence: medium; current-management reachability is bounded by mode and visibility scope. - Caveats: live registry is process-local and excludes isolated child processes; job roster is distinct from the live-run registry. A message is not guaranteed to be obeyed; parent job control is a particular attended background-job mode, not global project governance.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped complementary, sufficiently independent audit of actual operating claims with a corrective return to S3 was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misleading task results or unchecked work quality remain possible; ordinary testing and model verification do not implement an independent organizational audit route. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the same working agent can run verifyEdits, read tool errors and retry; developers run an offline selfcheck. No distinct audit owner decides claims against independent reality for the operating runtime. - Decision owner: none within the declared S3 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordinary edit verification, session transcripts, tool status, deterministic development tests, job progress and human approval prompts. - Closure path: local results return to their producing task&#39;s S1; no independently sourced audit finding → responsible S3 corrective decision circuit is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: source/test checks are valuable but either ordinary production verification or adjacent development evidence, not a complementary management audit. - Evidence: src/client/agent.ts, src/client/live.ts, src/selfcheck.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: downstream users can create their own independent audit; those additions are outside this frozen first-party distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: investigate a newly connected external tool environment and construct future reusable operating capabilities tailored to it. - Disturbance / variety regulated: newly available MCP integrations and their unfamiliar tool schemas, future tasks requiring capabilities Ada has not yet encoded as skills. - External distinction: names, descriptions and parameter schemas of tools belonging to the sole configured external MCP integration. - Future / prospective distinction: model-generated map of 4–8 integration capability areas beyond the current request and reusable procedures for later sessions. - Adaptation option generated: per-capability first-party SKILL.md recipes drafted by delegated agents and placed in .ada/skills. - Path back into current capability / S3: toolsmith reads integration docs, prompts model for capability areas, delegates future-oriented skills, persists validated-frontmatter drafts, and subsequent normal project skill loading can expose those procedures to the operating agent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model chooses which external capabilities merit reusable skills and delegates their content drafting; storage utilities implement that decision. - Decision owner: model-backed toolsmith orchestration and separate drafting child models, within the configured external-integration boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: soleIntegration, readIntegrationDocs, spawnSub, writeProjectSkills, filesystem skills loader and MCP tool schemas. - Closure path: external integration schemas → model&#39;s prospective capability grouping → drafted skill options → persisted project skills → later agent can load/use the new capability guidance. - Boundary reachability: the registered toolsmith strategy runs within the shipped Agent, reads actually configured MCP tools and writes skills into the standard runtime&#39;s skill directory. This does not rely on contributor-only examples or offline tests for ownership. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no deterministic list fixes which capability areas or skill instructions to create; model discretion supplies the prospective adaptation judgment. The storage path only enforces the selected capability changes. - Evidence: src/client/agent.ts, src/client/cli.ts, src/selfcheck.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural; first-party deterministic stub MCP/schema and skill-persistence checks. - Confidence: medium-high for the conditional one-integration mode. - Caveats: prospective skills are not proven effective; this requires exactly one connected integration, user configuration and successful model outputs. General plan/task decomposition and memory alone were not credited as S4.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party closed identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and authoritative return to operations at the Ada runtime recursion was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user and enterprise permission choices can constrain task execution, but no identity-level dispute or ethos trade-off is handled inside a shipped S5 loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operators/enterprise admins choose model defaults, tool permissions, seats and role configuration; no exposed first-party path escalates an identity/ultimate-policy issue to an ultimate authority and feeds its adjudication back as such. - Decision owner: external user or enterprise administrator for configuration and ordinary policies, not an established S5 owner for this assessed system. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/server/enterprise.ts seat/policy endpoints, src/client/cli.ts org policy fetch/cache and approval flow, tool permission gates. - Closure path: configuration reaches later tool enforcement, but the required identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority decision → returned identity resolution circuit is absent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static permissions, generic human approval and administrator-issued defaults are not, without a specific ultimate-policy decision, S5 governance closure. - Evidence: src/server/enterprise.ts, src/client/cli.ts, src/client/tools.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: governance of an enterprise or OSS project running Ada is a different recursion and cannot be imported merely because those humans operate the software.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f50ef932fa46899990b1e816a162a161c7035bd3/assessments/ad/ada.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f50ef932fa46899990b1e816a162a161c7035bd3\">f50ef93 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-07T13:44:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Menfre01/waveloom\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Waveloom</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-07\">2026-10-07</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Menfre01/waveloom/tree/daccab5d4816803a82eb6eabfe44a68c75bc1f58\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision daccab5d4816803a82eb6eabfe44a68c75bc1f58\">daccab5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding work by inspecting the workspace, editing/writing files, running commands/tests, using external information tools, and iterating from tool results toward task completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty in source state, task requirements, tool/test results, errors, and evolving conversation/workspace context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next tool/action, interpret results, retry/change strategy, delegate work, and decide when the requested operation is complete. - Decision owner: the parent Waveloom coding agent; delegated fork agents can own bounded subordinate operational work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Think-Act-Observe loop, tool registry, permission guard, sandbox, hooks, todo reminders, context compaction, session persistence, error backoff, background-task notifications. - Closure path: user request → model decision → first-party tool/subagent action → returned result/event → next model decision → further action or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: the shipped loop directly registers/executes first-party coding tools and receives their outputs; the supported agent tool creates nested first-party loops from that same runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses and sequences operational actions from available tools and changes behavior from observed results. Deterministic runtime layers execute and constrain those choices rather than replacing the discretionary operation. - Evidence: pkg/agentloop/loop.go, pkg/agentloop/execute.go, pkg/prompt/default.md. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: individual write/execute actions may require human approval depending on active permission policy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among distinct delegated S1 units by deciding which work can safely proceed in parallel and which dependency-bearing work must remain serial, then reintegrating subordinate results into the parent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting/dependent delegated work, including the risk that tasks with ordering dependencies or overlapping operational scope execute concurrently and invalidate one another&#39;s assumptions/results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the parent agent chooses subtask boundaries, selects parallel versus serial execution, launches multiple first-party agents together for independent work, and consumes their returned results before continuing dependent work. - Decision owner: the parent Waveloom agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent tool marked ConcurrentSafe; fork/cold subagent construction; parent todo lifecycle; returned tool results; system-prompt coordination rules. - Distinct S1 units: first-party fork/cold subagents instantiated as separate model-backed operational loops under one parent session. - Inter-S1 disturbance: dependency-bearing delegated work can invalidate assumptions or duplicate/conflict with peer work if run concurrently; writable fork work also creates concrete shared-workspace collision risk when scopes are not independent. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent agent is instructed to partition independent work into parallel agent calls and keep dependent work sequential, with scoped prompts and parent-managed task lifecycle. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each subagent&#39;s final result is returned as a parent agent tool result; the parent then updates task status and uses those results to decide dependent follow-up, repair, or completion. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the parallel-versus-serial decision is explicitly tied to whether subordinate S1 work is independent or dependency-constrained, regulating concrete interference among concurrently viable operational units rather than merely transporting prompts. - Closure path: parent identifies independent/dependent subtasks → launches independent agent calls together or serializes dependent work → subordinate S1 units execute → each final result returns as a tool result → parent updates task state and decides subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: the coordination policy is in the shipped default system prompt and the model-visible first-party agent tool implements concurrent subordinate execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime can execute concurrent tool calls, but it does not decide which work is independent or dependency-constrained. That discretionary partitioning/ordering right is assigned to the parent model. - Evidence: pkg/prompt/default.md, pkg/subagent/agent.go, pkg/agentloop/execute.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic concurrent tools do not count by themselves; the positive mapping depends on the shipped agent-owned dependency/parallelization policy plus subordinate-agent return path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently complete whole-current control loop over all active operational units/resources/commitments is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an S3 disturbance at this recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none meeting S3 closure. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: todo list/status, step/error limits, background-task registry/kill tool, permission/sandbox state, subagent events shown to the TUI, session statistics. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent can plan/delegate and later consume subagent results, but subagent execution is a synchronous tool boundary from the parent&#39;s perspective; streamed subagent events are routed for UI visibility rather than exposed as a whole-current model control surface with live steer/kill/reprioritize rights across subordinate agents. Background shell tasks have a kill path, but they are tool processes rather than a whole-system S1 control plane. - Evidence: pkg/subagent/agent.go, pkg/tool/killbackgroundtask.go, pkg/session/sessionpersist.go. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: todo/task tracking supports coordination and persistence, but task labels/status alone are not whole-current S3 control.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review or adversarially verify the focal coding operation using fresh-context read-only agents and return findings to the operating parent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false confidence, regressions, security defects, test/build failures, and implementation mistakes that the focal coding path may overlook. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a cold evaluate or verification agent independently inspects/tests the repository and produces an assessment or PASS/FAIL verdict; the parent receives that output and decides corrective follow-up before completion. - Decision owner: the cold review/verification agent owns the audit judgment; the parent agent owns subsequent operational correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cold context construction, project-write tools removed from the cold registry, read-only subagent shell, dedicated evaluate/verification prompts, returned agent tool result, subagent transcript/events. - Claim being audited: the parent coding agent&#39;s claim that a substantive implementation/change is correct and ready to report as complete. - Ordinary reporting path: the parent agent&#39;s own tool results, implementation reasoning, local build/test attempts, and completion narrative. - Complementary access path: a separate cold evaluate/verification model directly reads the repository and, for verification, runs builds/tests/adversarial checks under a read-only project boundary. - Independence boundary: fresh cold model context without inherited parent conversation, project write/edit tools removed, dedicated review/verification instructions, and an explicit independent assessment or PASS/FAIL verdict returned to the parent. - Who acts on findings: the parent coding agent receives the review/verdict as a tool result and is instructed to repair failed verification or re-check before claiming completion. - Closure path: parent coding work/change → parent invokes cold evaluate/verification → fresh agent directly inspects repository and/or runs tests/adversarial checks → review/verdict returned as parent tool result → parent context incorporates finding → parent can fix/re-verify or report completion. - Boundary reachability: evaluate/verification are accepted values of the shipped model-visible agent tool; their registries and dedicated prompts are constructed in first-party code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit conclusion is generated by a distinct cold model invocation with direct evidence access, not by deterministic logging or the same parent reasoning trace. - Evidence: pkg/subagent/agent.go, pkg/prompt/default.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a supported agent-invoked audit mode, not a guarantee that every coding turn always invokes it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no complete first-party external/future adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web tools, AGENTS.md/project memory, skills/plugins, provider/model configuration, compaction, session persistence, environment probing. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external information can be gathered for a current coding task, and configuration can change available capability, but there is no first-party loop that scans the external/future environment, generates adaptation options, selects an adaptation, and changes the harness&#39;s continuing capability/policy. - Evidence: pkg/prompt/default.md, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: web research for the current task remains S1 support unless it closes prospective adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompt, permission rules, sandbox policy, plan approval, hooks, configuration, bypass mode, user approval dialogs. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these surfaces constrain or authorize individual actions and modes; they do not frame identity/ultimate-policy questions, route them to a legitimate S5 authority, and return authoritative governance decisions into subsequent operation. - Evidence: pkg/permission/types.go, pkg/agentloop/loop.go, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user plan approval and tool permission approval are operational governance boundaries, not S5 merely because a human is authoritative over an action.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/67b96b607f46c200a90e92a240e0bdb39f8d1475/assessments/waveloom.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 67b96b607f46c200a90e92a240e0bdb39f8d1475\">67b96b6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T20:29:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/helpofai/HelpOfAi-Cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HelpOfAi</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/helpofai/HelpOfAi-Cli/tree/a102dfbd043b41688b155135fb9bd48ebb7a3113\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a102dfbd043b41688b155135fb9bd48ebb7a3113\">a102dfb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform coding work by selecting, executing and revising model/tool actions against a live workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, changing files, shell/build/test failures, provider/tool errors, long-running goals, context limits, task uncertainty and user/sandbox constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next coding/tool/delegation action from current evidence, revise strategy after failures, and decide when the current operational objective is complete or blocked. - Decision owner: the active model-backed HelpOfAi coding agent or fleet worker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: core agent loop, tool catalog/execution, persistent goal loop, sessions, context management, file/shell/search/git tools, subagents, skills, sandbox/approval and provider routing. - Closure path: objective/context → model selects action/tool → runtime executes → concrete workspace/tool evidence returns → model revises subsequent work until completion, blocking, cancellation or configured budget exhaustion. - Boundary reachability: ordinary TUI and helpofai exec directly instantiate the model/tool runtime; persistent Goal and fleet worker paths reuse that same supported runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor the runtime still exposes tools, persistence and enforcement, but open-ended coding decisions and strategy revision disappear. - Evidence: README; docs/AGENTRUNTIME.md; crates/tui/src/goalloop.rs; crates/tui/src/core/engine/toolexecution.rs; crates/tui/src/tools/subagent/mod.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external providers supply inference but do not own the product&#39;s operational coding loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among distinct current worker S1 units competing for bounded Fleet execution capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous worker demand can exceed configured per-run, per-host or per-task-class capacity; stale leases and interrupted workers can also retain execution claims that would otherwise interfere with later work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the shipped Fleet scheduler applies configured worker/host/task-class ceilings, lease validity, heartbeat/retry policy and queued-work eligibility to decide which worker slots may be launched or recovered. - Decision owner: constructor/runtime policy. The scheduler deterministically executes configured coordination policy; it does not itself make autonomous semantic tradeoffs among workers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Fleet ledger, leases, heartbeats, maxworkersperrun, maxworkersperhost, maxworkerspertaskclass, stale-worker recovery, launch admission, retry/restart state and worker lifecycle events. - Closure path: current worker occupancy/lease state + queued tasks → scheduler evaluates coordination policy → eligible work is launched/restarted while excess/conflicting work remains queued or stale claims are recovered → updated worker/ledger state is visible on the next scheduler tick. - Boundary reachability: Agent Fleet is a shipped local-first runtime mode and FleetScheduler is directly used by the first-party fleet control plane; no custom coordinator implementation is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination loop is real and closed, but its decisive capacity/lease rules are configured deterministic policy; therefore the state is constructor C, not A. - Evidence: docs/FLEET.md; docs/AGENTRUNTIME.md; crates/tui/src/fleet/scheduler.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the higher-level fleet-manager agent performs S3 intervention; S2 here is specifically the deterministic peer-capacity/lease coordination relation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: manage a current fleet run as a whole by diagnosing worker state and choosing current interventions over active commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stale or failed workers, transient transport/provider failures, verifier disagreement, task failures, retry exhaustion, unsafe continuation and ambiguous current ownership/escalation needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: classify a current worker failure and choose among restart, interrupt/stop, preserve/no-retry, or escalation based on typed run/worker/artifact evidence and current authority boundaries. - Decision owner: the model-backed manager agent using the shipped fleet-manager system skill; deterministic Fleet controls execute and ledger the chosen intervention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled fleet-manager skill, fleet status, inspect, logs, artifacts, restart, interrupt, stop, Runtime API controls, retry budgets and Fleet ledger. - Closure path: whole-run status + worker/artifact evidence → manager agent classifies current condition → manager selects typed intervention or escalation → Fleet runtime applies/records action → subsequent worker/run state changes and is observable through the same typed surfaces. - Boundary reachability: crates/tui/assets/skills/fleet-manager/SKILL.md is a bundled first-party system skill and the referenced Fleet CLI/API controls are shipped runtime surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the manager model leaves deterministic scheduler/restart primitives but removes the semantic diagnosis of transient/task/verifier/needs-human state and the discretionary choice of safe current intervention. - Evidence: crates/tui/assets/skills/fleet-manager/SKILL.md; docs/FLEET.md; Fleet status/control runtime surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: scheduler-only operation would support constructor-level current control; A relies on the supported manager-agent mode that closes the model-owned current-control loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review code or change evidence through a model-backed reviewer path distinct from the producing coding actor&#39;s ordinary report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: defects, missing tests, correctness/security/reliability issues and unsupported completion claims that the producing actor may overlook. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect resolved file/diff/PR evidence under a dedicated reviewer prompt and decide which issues/suggestions/overall assessment are materially warranted. - Decision owner: the separate model call instantiated by the first-party review tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only review source resolver, dedicated senior-reviewer system prompt, separate LLM request with no tools, structured JSON findings, plus optional deterministic runverifiers evidence. - Closure path: producer changes workspace/reports progress → review tool directly resolves file/diff/PR evidence → separate reviewer model returns structured findings → parent coding/manager actor receives findings and can revise work or create corrective action. - Boundary reachability: review is a shipped first-party agent tool in crates/tui/src/tools/review.rs; it does not depend on repository CI or an external review service. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the reviewer model leaves source collection and schemas but removes the semantic judgment that identifies and prioritizes issues. - Evidence: crates/tui/src/tools/review.rs; crates/tui/src/tools/verifier.rs; docs/SUBAGENTS.md; agent prompt verification guidance. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: runverifiers itself is deterministic and is not the source of the A state; the positive claim rests on the separate reviewer LLM path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: durable memory, project context, skills, provider switching and codebase-memory can improve current or later coding work, but they primarily retain/reuse information or configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped first-party path was found that autonomously distinguishes future environmental change, develops adaptation options for the HelpOfAi harness itself and returns a selected adaptation into present capability. - Decision owner: not established at S4 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: global/project memory, remember, project context/cache, skills, MCP/provider configuration, codebase memory and software/project facts. - Closure path: persisted memory/context/skills are loaded into later prompts, but there is no separate outside-and-then option-generation/adaptation cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retention, retrieval and configurable skills do not satisfy S4 without a future/external distinction and adaptation-option closure. - Evidence: docs/MEMORY.md; crates/tui/src/memory.rs; project context and skills surfaces; HELPOfAi-CODEBASE-MEMORY.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the aios/enterprise/learning-engine material is declarative/specification content and is not imported as runtime evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: HelpOfAi has global/project constitutions, protected invariants, authority ordering, branch/verification policy, escalation conditions, permissions, sandboxing and trust/capability policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: these mechanisms enforce or inject operator-authored policy, but no first-party runtime path was found that autonomously or parent-governedly resolves an identity/ultimate-policy issue and returns a durable authoritative identity decision into system operation. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: global constitution, .helpofai/constitution.json, authority ordering, escalatewhen, approval hooks, sandbox policy, fleet trust/capability grants and operator configuration. - Closure path: configured policy constrains current action and can force user escalation, but the reviewed runtime does not itself close an identity-level policy deliberation/update loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a static or operator-authored constitution can be highly authoritative without itself being a VSM S5 decision process; permissions and escalation triggers are likewise insufficient alone. - Evidence: README nested-constitution section; project-context constitution loader; sandbox/approval docs; Fleet trust-policy documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: escalatewhen creates a path to human authority for selected operational conflicts, but the frozen evidence does not show a durable identity-policy issue → authority decision → changed governing identity closure rather than ordinary approval/escalation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/de6d21fbe1096ec9bee2b871ebcb0ce625db0e04/assessments/helpofai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit de6d21fbe1096ec9bee2b871ebcb0ce625db0e04\">de6d21f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-15T00:59:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PyModel/pythinker-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pythinker Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-15\">2026-10-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PyModel/pythinker-code/tree/5c5a2053116c1e18c7e1e22710d846fc27945f08\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5c5a2053116c1e18c7e1e22710d846fc27945f08\">5c5a205 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: turn an uncertain coding objective into environment-responsive file inspection, edits, command execution and task results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source state, compiler/test errors, tool failures and unexpected repository conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select coding/investigation tools, modify source and change next steps after observing returned results; in Tower mode individual model workers own bounded mission execution. - Decision owner: first-party main and child model-directed Agent Core runs. External LLM transport supplies inference but not a separate borrowed coding-agent executable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: loop machine/tool scheduler, tool registry, permissions, model adapter, context and session history, optional subagent profiles. - Closure path: user&#39;s goal/mission and observed workspace state → model-chosen tool action → first-party executor with authorization → tool result/error returned into the subsequent model turn → revised action or completion. - Boundary reachability: shipped main runtime runs the agent loop, and SessionSubagentService instantiates first-party background and foreground agent runs. Tower workers use this shipped subagent path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model-led action selection leaves deterministic tools and scheduling, but no equivalent coding decisions. - Evidence: packages/agent-core-v2/src/agent/loop/machine/engine.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/agent/toolExecutor/toolExecutor.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/session/subagent/subagent.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/agent/tools/agent/agent.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concrete interference between distinct concurrently tasked, first-party coding workers in one shared repository under enabled Tower mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two workers taking overlapping writable scope, git branch identity collisions, and incompatible commits/files being integrated into the same base. - Decisive decision or feedback right: deterministic mission admission rejects pairwise conflicting scopes; isolated branch/worktree allocation constrains writes; merge admission refuses out-of-scope changes and reports actual overlap with other unmerged branches. Leader/worker receives the specific constraint or conflict for reallocation/rebase. - Decision owner: first-party TowerStore and worktree/merge protocol as constructor, with agent tower leader owning discretionary redesign of missions. No claim that the deterministic scope match or git conflict test is model-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mission scope stems and dependency graph, unique branch identifiers, worktree creation, assertScopesDisjoint, git diffs, branch merge checks, protocol results. - Closure path: proposed mission overlaps another S1&#39;s scope → rejected allocation requiring the tower to revise mission ownership; or child branch changes → merge checks/report → conflicting worker is instructed to rebase/resolve and re-review before successful integration. - Boundary reachability: flagged first-party TowerFeature registers TowerPlan/TowerSpawn/TowerMerge and these operations invoke TowerStore on actual git worktrees. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model may plan better scopes, but source-specific anti-interference controls and gates are deterministic and remain if the planner&#39;s discretion is removed; classify as C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: model-backed Tower worker agents with separate missions, prompts, worktrees and changed-file outputs. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping or incompatible writes competing for one project/base branch. - Attenuating coordination relation: pairwise scope exclusivity plus worktree isolation and conflict-scoped merge admission. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: failed mission scope admission and merge conflict details flow back into tower control, which can redefine ownership, ask rebases or hold the merge. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: scope collision and merge interference are keyed to the cross-worker conflict itself; they are not just a message bus or a sequential task queue. - Evidence: packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/protocol/store.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/plan/plan.md; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/spawn/spawn.md; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/merge/mergeTool.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high (for enabled Tower mode) - Caveats: overlap detection of declared patterns uses scope stems rather than formal all-glob intersection; does not claim absolute prevention of every edit conflict. The feature is opt-in and marked experimental.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the whole set of current Tower missions and role agents with assignment, priority/dependency control, rework and base-merge decisions at the shared objective boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete missions, diverging child outcomes, blocked dependencies, unreviewed branches, failed audits and conflicting current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the model-led main Tower agent chooses the plan/decomposition of the shared goal, worker/reviewer assignment, review/rework routing and whether to request a merge when prerequisites are met. - Decision owner: model-directed Tower lead on the main-agent session, not the deterministic protocol store. Store and gates enforce the leader&#39;s permissible moves and track roster/status. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TowerInit/Plan/Spawn/Mission/Status/Inbox/Send/Review/Merge, mission index, dependency/scope records, agent task/background tools and merge gate. - Closure path: current mission/worker/review statuses → tower model replans/assigns/reviews/escalates → worker instructions or held/merged branches → updated shared mission state and subsequent tower decisions. - Boundary reachability: TowerFeature bundles tools and exposes them to the model in an explicitly enabled shipped mode; TowerStore persists live cross-mission scope, owner, status and reviews; TowerMergeTool is main-agent-only. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without tower lead model choices, the protocol can reject bad calls but cannot choose appropriate decomposition, worker targets or corrective task allocation. The positive autonomy is the agent&#39;s current fleet decision, not the presence of a dashboard. - Whole-system current view: all active Tower mission scopes, agents, review rounds, dependencies, blockers and integration states for the shared project. - Current-control decision scope: plan or revise mission set, assign workers/reviewers, handle blocked/current work, instruct rework and select eligible merges/abandonments. - Evidence: packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/towerFeature.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/plan/plan.md; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/mission/missionTool.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/status/statusTool.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/spawn/spawn.md; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/protocol/store.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: enabled Tower project scope only. Ordinary background subagent delegation without Tower is not enough for S3. No separate parent-owned (P) state is proven.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge a worker&#39;s claimed completed branch against independently inspected changed code, mission scope and acceptance tasks, with a mandatory corrective return before integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missed task criteria, code defects, unsupported “done” claims and post-review branch mutation. - Claim being audited: a mission owner claims its branch has implemented the assigned tasks within scope and is ready for integration into the shared base. - Ordinary reporting path: the coding worker marks mission tasks complete and requests review; ordinary worker files, commits and task progress originate from the actor performing implementation. - Complementary access path: TowerSpawn reviewtarget creates a separately running reviewer-role agent with the mission task list and reviewed branch/diff; its TowerReview result is bound to the observed git commit and written as an independent review record. - Independence boundary: an assigned reviewer identity is distinct from the assigned worker and must be registered against that target; TowerStore.submitReview rejects review submissions by other non-tower actors. No assertion is made that this source review provides external independent organization-level GitHub approval. - Who acts on findings: non-clean TowerReview reactivates a completed mission; the tower leader routes evidence back to its owner or a replacement and the merge gate forbids incorporation until a newer clean verdict on the latest branch tip. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately spawned reviewer model can issue clean versus defect/hold verdict with findings through TowerReview; the actual latest review status and commit identity determine admission to the base. - Decision owner: dedicated model-running reviewer agent for semantic source inspection and critique, separate from the coding worker. Deterministic TowerStore enforces eligibility and exact-tip freshness, and lead model/worker subsequently owns repairs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TowerSpawn(reviewtarget) independent briefing with authored mission/tasks, reviewer-only TowerStore.submitReview permission, persistent review file including target/reviewer/tip, MAXREVIEWROUNDS and merge hard gate. - Closure path: model worker completes branch → separate assigned reviewer reads branch/diff and submits evidence/critique → non-clean review reactivates completed mission and prevents merge → main tower routes findings to original/replacement worker → changed branch must be re-reviewed at its new tip → clean verdict enables merge. - Boundary reachability: reviewer spawning and review/merge tools are bundled within the same first-party TowerFeature; TowerStore.submitReview checks reviewer assignment and TowerStore.merge rejects absent/non-clean/stale review before git integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer model decides semantic defect severity and claims, while the store merely enforces that a recorded clean review from assigned actor is fresh. Removing reviewer discretion leaves no comparable real source-specific quality judgment, even though deterministic gate persists. - Evidence: packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/spawn/spawn.md; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/review/review.md; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/review/reviewTool.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/protocol/store.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/tools/merge/mergeTool.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high (for enabled Tower mode) - Caveats: the source verifies a reviewer identity and a submitted verdict, not the accuracy of all reviewer claims. Tower itself can also submit a review; this positive independent-actor claim refers specifically to first-party assigned reviewer-subagent mode rather than self-review by tower. It does not imply external organizational independent-review gate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct shipped prospective outside-environment sensing → future capability options → adoption into current organizational capability has been established for the assessed organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future tool/method changes, emerging project needs and external constraints, beyond the present operator-authored coding mission. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agents can research a present coding task, the operator can configure providers, plugins and future goals, but no first-party S4 owner of system-level prospective adaptation choice is identified. - Decision owner: no qualifying S4-specific decision owner on the inspected first-party standard path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goal mode, cron, sessions/compaction, user/project skills, plugins, external web tools and long-running task loops. - Closure path: task information affects current coding actions or a user-supplied later goal; no separate outside/future options/adoption decision circuit is demonstrated as a system-owned return to current Tower management. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sustained task execution and session memory are still S1/current task support. Goal deadlines and timers do not choose a prospectively adaptive capability portfolio. - Evidence: packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/goal/goalOps.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/goal/goalDeadlineScheduler.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/skill/skillOps.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/agent/contextMemory/contextMemory.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime constitutional identity or ultimate-purpose dispute → legitimate authority decision → returned ongoing policy-governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dangerous commands, permission policies, privacy and user-requested objectives can create authorization problems but do not necessarily concern ultimate mission/identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator/permission policy approves or blocks actions; Tower lead can change mission scope or abandon one, not authoritatively redefine the organization&#39;s ultimate purpose/identity under a legitimate parent resolution. - Decision owner: user and local tool-permission policy for individual actions; none identified for a first-party S5-specific ultimate identity decision/return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool approval, permission modes, model instructions, project-scoped tools/skills, Tower ownership limits and safety prompts. - Closure path: permission decisions affect tool execution and Tower decisions affect work planning, but no actual identity-level issue has a first-party legitimate authority and returned constitutional rule governing later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the presence of human confirmation or authorized Tower lead discretion does not transfer constitutional policy rights to the model or a deterministic gate. - Evidence: packages/agent-core-v2/src/agent/permissionPolicy/permissionPolicy.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/agent/interaction/approval.ts; packages/agent-core-v2/src/features/tower/protocol/store.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5ffa18aae2f174cf1cde20c073ca41c93285b954/assessments/pythinker-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5ffa18aae2f174cf1cde20c073ca41c93285b954\">5ffa18a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T20:37:48+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/huiliyi37/Tianshu-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tianshu Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/huiliyi37/Tianshu-harness/tree/6dbf1e0482cf5338b7587d029cbfadc3306b05f5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6dbf1e0482cf5338b7587d029cbfadc3306b05f5\">6dbf1e0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute uncertain repository coding work by deciding files/tools/commands and next actions after observing real outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source state, inconsistent tool outcomes, failing verification, incomplete edits and changed runtime context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root and subordinate working models decide next tool use/code change and whether the coding subtask is done after tool-result feedback. - Decision owner: operating coding models in root and write-capable delegated S1 workers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/tool loop, command and file tools, worker tool whitelist, permission hooks, context persistence and verification gates. - Closure path: model selection → tool admission and file/shell execution → observed result/error → further model action/repair/final answer. - Boundary reachability: first-party packaged CLI/runtime invokes root coding actions and dispatched write-worker model sessions; no third-party workflow must be authored. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model&#39;s discretionary next-action choice leaves tool dispatch/verification plumbing but no task-specific adaptive coding operation. - Evidence: README.en.md; loop.ts; coordinator.ts; coordination-policy.ts; worker-write-gate.ts - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate inter-S1 file-write interference among concurrently running implementation workers through scope-dependent dispatch admission and returned conflict feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two independently running write-capable S1 workers attempting to change overlapping files, including across separate waves and single vs batch delegation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the deterministic Coordinator global file-claim gate rejects overlapping incoming orders after slot admission; the blocked result and suggested scope fix are returned to the caller. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic coordination constructor, not a demonstrated agent-owned S2 choice. Root model may change subsequent assignments after returned conflict but deterministic gate enforces the actual conflict relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: declared scope.files, work-order queue dependency/file checks, current in-flight claims map, concurrency semaphore, separate read-vs-write worker profiles. - Closure path: existing writer claims files → second writer&#39;s overlapping scope detected → second order blocked with conflicting paths and wait/narrow guidance → caller may retry with revised scope once conflict clears; claims removed on settlement. - Boundary reachability: single delegation, batch and background dispatch use the shipped runDelegationWithGlobalGateImpl; it is wired before worker execution, not merely a test utility. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without model discretion the deterministic interference guard still rejects overlapping writers and reports the conflict; the model&#39;s possible broader rescoping is an S3/S1 scheduling response, not evidence that S2 itself becomes A. - Evidence: coordinator.ts; work-queue.ts; coordination-policy.ts; team-orchestrate.ts - Basis: explicit executable dispatch code - Confidence: high within declared file-scope contract; unscoped writers blocked rather than assumed safe - Caveats: file scope must be declared and truthful, and this does not guarantee detection of semantic collisions between disjoint files. - Distinct S1 units: separately running write-capable implementation workers executing model/tool tasks under coordinator work orders. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping write scopes could corrupt or overwrite another worker&#39;s changed files. - Attenuating coordination relation: global scope claim admission rejects unsafe concurrent work and serializes non-read-only conflicts. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: blocked worker result with conflict paths and revised scope/wait instruction returned to manager/lead to modify later task dispatch. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the gate compares specific shared write resources between S1 workers and rejects the conflicting action; it is not merely a queue/status list.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: exercise current management of multiple delegated coding operations through model-selected work breakdown, wave assignments and response to current worker evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple simultaneously running task commitments, dependency and deadline/verification failures, blocked workers and available execution capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead model elects how to break down the present user objective, requests worker profiles/scopes and follow-on repairs or new work; wave execution/worker feedback enables dynamic commitments. - Decision owner: root/lead coding model in supported multi-worker team/plan modes; not TaskBoard read-only projection, deterministic queue or automatic review gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: teamorchestrate and plan-wave execution, queue state and live work results, dispatch gate, human plan approval where configured, verification/revision feedback. - Closure path: current multi-worker progress/failure/conflict/verification outcome → lead sees results and chooses updated task(s) or repair scope → coordinator dispatches a changed worker commitment → new status/review feedback. - Boundary reachability: shipped teamorchestrate tool and delegatebatch/coordinator paths run through the packaged runtime and are referenced by documented /team mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: remove the lead&#39;s discretionary plan/task selection and generic wave execution can still run a static plan, but cannot choose new task objectives, worker scopes or repair responses based on the present system view. - Evidence: team-orchestrate.ts; plan-executor.ts; coordinator.ts; task-board.ts; work-queue.ts; loop.ts - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: medium-high for session/workflow-scoped lead control, not multi-deployment authority - Caveats: some execution ordering is constructor-owned; operator-approved plans and predefined worker budgets remain human/deterministic controls. - Whole-system current view: the supported orchestration run aggregates per-wave work orders, worker status/results, verification and affected file scopes. - Current-control decision scope: select which current engineering subtask is assigned to which worker, when repair/revision is required, and what follow-up committed work to dispatch.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementing-agent completion evidence with a separate read-only test/file-reading goal judge and return a corrective verdict to the running goal-continuation controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a producing coding agent can falsely claim that a goal and individual acceptance criteria have been satisfied despite defects or missing behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately spawned goaljudge model directly verifies acceptance criteria with tests/files (optional browser/API where enabled) and classifies verified/rejected/inconclusive; a rejected verdict within retry budget causes the root task to continue and repair. - Decision owner: separate judge model for complementary verdict, with runtime controller for bounded verdict/retry disposition and implementing model for corrective work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goal tracker, coordinator delegate kind: verify, profile: goaljudge, read-only-plus-test profile, evidence snapshot and telemetry, bounded retry counter. - Closure path: implementer emits GOAL ACHIEVED → goal continuation creates independent judge order → judge directly inspects repository/test reality rather than trusting claim → verdict returns to controller → rejection yields explicit next-turn unmet criteria and repair work. - Boundary reachability: loop-factory.ts wires active goalJudge configuration and a coordinator reference directly to goal-continuation.ts; this is first-party runtime code, not an offline maintainer/CI reviewer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the distinct judge model exercises evidentiary judgment; deterministic verification-only gates and producer self-report cannot supply the same independent verdict. Optional fail-open on judge unavailability is explicitly disclosed. - Evidence: goal-judge.ts; goal-continuation.ts; loop-factory.ts; coordinator.ts; worker-evidence.ts; worker-write-gate.ts - Basis: explicit first-party source - Confidence: medium-high on reachable positive path; independent context vs implementation task, not a separate organization or universally enforced gate - Caveats: disabled/missing coordinator, inconclusive verdict or exhausted retries may accept an unverified completion with warning; no blanket fail-closed or S3 organizational audit claim. - Claim being audited: implementer&#39;s assertion that the user&#39;s goal and acceptance criteria are genuinely complete. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer summary, captured evidence/tool output and main-side write verification metadata. - Complementary access path: separately run read-only goaljudge worker instructed to use real file reads/tests to challenge each criterion, optionally HTTP/browser checks. - Independence boundary: fresh delegated model worker role/context inspecting actual project state rather than solely consuming producer&#39;s successful status. - Who acts on findings: goal continuation returns specific rejected criteria for further root implementation within bounded review rounds.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidence-backed shipped agent-owned prospective outside-world capability redesign/adoption loop changes the organization after external sensing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing model/provider capabilities, outside tools and future environmental opportunities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no operational prospective adaptation decision owner with an applied capability change and returned control closure established. - Decision owner: human/configuration for domain and model settings, coding agent for present-work choices; shadow-only routing model produces no applied decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cognitive hooks, model routing shadow telemetry, historical reward summaries, domain drift suggestion, task retrospective/memory and optional external web/tools. - Closure path: observations and model recommendations are stored or used in present tasks, but the checked model-routing-shadow controller explicitly never changes active model calls and domain drift detector only advises. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-context research, prompt/skill updates and shadow candidate rankings do not prove a future-oriented system adaptation decision with return. - Evidence: model-routing-shadow-controller.ts; domain-drift-detector.ts; retrospect.ts; README.en.md; loop-factory.ts - Basis: explicit no-apply code and bounded structural source inspection -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate identity/purpose-policy adjudicator and returned governing decision is shipped at this coding-engagement recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts over durable organizational identity, constitutional policy or mission rather than one dangerous tool/edit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no agent or first-party parent decides final mission identity and propagates a binding governing policy change. - Decision owner: human task owner/project maintainer outside system-in-focus sets purpose and governance; tool permission checks implement localized constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: security gates, worker whitelists, plan approvals, domain prompts, project rule memory and runtime hooks. - Closure path: human permission returns an allow/deny for an action, or a plan is approved; these do not constitute a constitutional identity conflict and an authoritative identity decision returned to operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safety policy engine and governance-like naming or project memory do not have the decisive ultimate policy/identity right. - Evidence: README.en.md; coordination-policy.ts; tool-preset.ts; loop.ts; plan-executor.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/89f1122e8c85715f8d7ecbbc86d0f8bfaaa740d1/assessments/tianshu-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 89f1122e8c85715f8d7ecbbc86d0f8bfaaa740d1\">89f1122 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-25T21:28:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentic-in/inferoa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inferoa</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentic-in/inferoa/tree/1fd9ec4518b26b60cc78cf67c4faf337fe9bb2cd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1fd9ec4518b26b60cc78cf67c4faf337fe9bb2cd\">1fd9ec4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: carry a long-horizon coding or research objective through repeated model/tool execution until a concrete environmental outcome is produced and evidence is recorded. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository state, ambiguous implementation/research findings, command/test/tool results, model/tool failures, context limits, resumed sessions, blockers and evidence discovered during execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive implementation/research/tool action from current objective, workspace and evidence, then update the persistent Loop state from the result. - Decision owner: the active Inferoa work agent/model during ordinary execution turns. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime/model gateway, tool registry, persistent SessionStore, Goal state, permissions, context compression, daemon lifecycle, managed worktrees and deterministic structural completion rules. - Closure path: objective enters /loop → execution model selects and performs workspace/research actions → tool/environment results return → agent updates step/coverage/frontier/evidence/risk state → later execution turns continue from the changed state until the horizon is exhausted and control passes to reflection. - Boundary reachability: /loop is the documented standard long-horizon product surface, and daemon goal mode runs the same Goal supervisor outside the foreground TUI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime machinery executes, persists and constrains actions, but removing the work agent eliminates the substantive choice of what to inspect, implement, benchmark, compare or verify next. - Evidence: loop-mode.md; supervisor-prompts.ts; supervisor.ts; daemon/supervisor.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 relation is established at the assessed Loop recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying inter-S1 interference/oscillation relation was established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S2 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session/worktree isolation, managed worktrees, subagent boundaries, daemon job lifecycle and merge preflight can prevent or contain execution interference, but they are not enough by themselves to establish S2. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed path reconstructs distinct S1 units → concrete inter-S1 disturbance → S2-specific attenuation → returned coordination feedback that changes subsequent S1 behaviour. - Distinct S1 units: the durable Loop itself is the established primary S1 at this recursion. Bounded subagents/verifier jobs are delegated execution/checking helpers and are not promoted to separate S1 units merely because they run in child sessions or worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: not established at the declared recursion. Potential repository conflicts between independently created worktrees are generic implementation risk unless a first-party organizational coordination relation around multiple credited S1s is shown. - Attenuating coordination relation: managed worktree isolation and merge preflight are strong containment/enforcement mechanisms, but the reviewed standard path does not tie them to a qualifying inter-S1 coordination conversation with feedback into multiple S1 behaviours. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: not established for an S2-specific relation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is not mapped as S2. The assessment intentionally refuses to count child spawning, session isolation, job queues or worktrees as coordination without the Profile witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no S2 function is established, so autonomy classification does not proceed. - Evidence: subagents.ts; subagent-tool.ts; worktree.ts; workers.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the Loop&#39;s current whole-objective commitments, priorities and continuation state across execution horizons using objective-level evidence rather than merely continue the current local plan. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local-plan exhaustion, incomplete objective coverage, unresolved frontier candidates, residual risk, runtime minimums, structural completion debt, missing verification and blockers that can make the current local horizon insufficient for the whole objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently choose expand, done or blocked for the current Loop horizon and, when expanding, define the next concrete work commitments against the original objective. - Decision owner: the autonomous model actor in the internal reflection request class. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent Goal state, coverage/frontier/evidence/risk ledgers, candidate and structural completion gates, runtime minimum, deterministic supervisor transitions, verification policy and daemon lifecycle. - Closure path: S1 execution updates persistent whole-objective state → supervisor invokes a separate internal reflection turn → reflection actor reconstructs objective decomposition and chooses expand/done/blocked → runtime validates structural/frontier/runtime/verifier constraints and persists the accepted transition → subsequent execution is expanded, paused/blocked or completed accordingly. - Boundary reachability: reflection is part of the standard /loop supervisor and daemon goal path; it is not a development-only evaluator. - Whole-system current view: the reflection prompt explicitly requires re-reading the top-level objective and reconciling coverage surfaces, uninspected surfaces, frontier items, rejected branches, residual risks, assumptions, delivery/research contract and executed evidence rather than treating the current checklist as the system boundary. - Current-control decision scope: whether the organization should continue current work, open a new horizon with concrete commitments, block for missing external authority/input or terminate the Loop as complete. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic gates can reject an unsafe/premature done and enforce invariants, but they do not independently generate the same discretionary expand/done/blocked judgment over the objective. Removing the reflection agent leaves rules but removes the substantive current-control choice and next-horizon design. - Evidence: supervisor-prompts.ts; supervisor.ts; goal-tools.ts; loop-mode.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the work agent&#39;s completion/correctness claims through a checker path with materially different authority and direct access to operational evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overclaimed completion, missing tests/evidence, implementation defects, security/privacy/permission risk, stale documentation, weak research conclusions and other failures that ordinary work-agent self-reporting may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue an independent structured verification verdict from direct evidence as a checker rather than implementer. - Decision owner: the verifier model actor in the dedicated verification request class / verifier role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifier-specific prompts/roles, read-only Goal tool restrictions, separate internal runs, optional separate child sessions/worktrees, verification records, verifier policy and completion gates. - Closure path: work agent/reflection presents completion candidate and evidence → verifier independently inspects loop state plus relevant files/diffs/commands/resources/tests → verifier records checker verdict → completion policy consumes that verdict → valid pass can permit completion; missing/non-passing evidence pauses or keeps the Loop from closing and further work can follow. - Boundary reachability: daemon goal supervision sets autoVerifyCompletion: true; first-party TUI/CLI verifier suites also expose multi-role verification in normal product operation. - Claim being audited: that the current bounded Loop work is sufficiently correct/evidenced to satisfy completion or a specified implementation/test/security/docs/research review concern. - Ordinary reporting path: the work agent updates goal step/coverage/frontier/evidence/residual-risk state and the reflection actor can propose done with verification evidence. - Complementary access path: verifier runs are instructed to inspect current state, recent evidence, relevant files, diffs, commands, resources and test results directly and to try to falsify completion rather than rely on assistant narrative. - Independence boundary: verification runs are restricted to goal inspection/verdict recording, are told not to implement or mutate the plan, and verifier suites can use separate child sessions and isolated worktrees with role-specific prompts. - Who acts on findings: the Goal supervisor/completion policy consumes verification records; failures or missing required passes prevent completion and return the organization to paused/additional-work paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime policy stores/enforces the verdict, but the evaluative judgment is produced by an autonomous verifier model from direct evidence. Removing the verifier actor leaves the gate but not the independent audit judgment. - Evidence: verifier.ts; verifier-suite.ts; goal-tools.ts; supervisor.ts; daemon/supervisor.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S4 organizational adaptation loop is established at the assessed Loop recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an outside-and-then organizational adaptation function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Discover loops can run experiments and compare hypotheses; scheduled discovery senses GitHub/local-git/HTTP/npm conditions; inbox candidates can be explicitly promoted to work; self-improve can turn verified historical Loop evidence into a staged skill proposal and explicit adoption can enable a learned skill. - Closure path: not applicable for S4; no reviewed standard path closes external/future sensing → development of organizational adaptation options → decisive adaptation judgment → returned change in present organizational capability/S3 as the system&#39;s S4 function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Discover mode is objective-local research, not automatically organizational adaptation. Scheduled discovery mostly converts external/current signals into work candidates, and promotion is an explicit control-plane action. Self-improve is evidence-driven learning with explicit adoption; the Profile explicitly does not equate generic learning/self-improvement with S4 without an external-and-prospective adaptation conversation. - Evidence: loop-mode.md; discovery.ts; inbox.ts; policy.ts; learning.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: a wider operator/workspace recursion could potentially organize discovery plus skill-adoption mechanisms into an S4 path. That wider organization would need its own boundary and evidence; it is not silently credited to one Loop objective.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the assessed Loop recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an identity/ultimate-policy matter. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established within the standard Loop boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user-supplied objective/preference/runtime/HIL policy, permission modes, unattended safety gates, approval surfaces, verifier policy, skill configuration and explicit learned-skill adoption constrain or configure operation but do not establish S5. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed path shows an identity/ultimate-policy issue reaching a legitimate ultimate authority and returning as authoritative policy governing subsequent Loop operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: work/reflection/verifier agents operate inside configured objective, safety and permission boundaries. They are not shown deciding the Loop organization&#39;s ultimate identity or policy. Human review/approval of a task, skill or external mutation is not promoted to S5 merely because the user has final say. - Evidence: policy.ts; loop-mode.md; goal-tools.ts; inbox.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: an operator can define objectives, review work and configure policy, but Methodology 0.3.6 requires identity-level closure rather than generic task/control authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/13205e9d979e023949c02852f01882e4eab1b89c/assessments/inferoa.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 13205e9d979e023949c02852f01882e4eab1b89c\">13205e9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T21:28:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gitlawb/zero\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zero</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gitlawb/zero/tree/99721c762f37cd43ac511007a5f51d1846df959e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 99721c762f37cd43ac511007a5f51d1846df959e\">99721c7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: take uncertain coding actions (repository inspection, file edits, command/test execution) in response to task goals and feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown codebase state, test failures, tool errors, partial patching and user-request changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses code actions, tool arguments, continuation/correction after observed results. - Decision owner: Zero&#39;s coding agent model (and delegated specialist model in its own worker context). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Go turn engine, command and file tools, durable session context, sandbox/permission gate, optional post-edit verifier. - Closure path: model tool call → first-party local read/edit/shell → tool/verification outcome re-enters turn history → model chooses repair, delegation or final response. - Boundary reachability: normal zero / zero exec supported pathways, not only test fixtures. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the current model&#39;s discretionary action selection, tool dispatch and verification only implement bounded mechanisms. - Evidence: README.md; loop.go; selfcorrect.go; tasktool.go; exec.go - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: simultaneously model-running teammate/subagent coding members launched via swarmspawn, each executing its own delegated task; a predecessor/successor pair is distinct S1 execution ownership when swarmhandoff transfers work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent members contend for a shared bounded set of team execution slots, and predecessor/successor actors can otherwise overlap execution of the same in-flight task during ownership transfer. - Attenuating coordination relation: the team&#39;s maximum-concurrency cap queues excess S1s and the handoff source-stop execution barrier forbids the successor from executing until the incumbent exits; failed/timed-out handoffs fail closed. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: pending tasks start only as slots free, and the successor receives the handoff briefing only after the source is stopped; coordinator status/results inform the manager&#39;s subsequent allocations. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: this is interference-specific control of competing independently model-running S1s for a shared capacity and exclusive ownership of a continuing task; mere mailbox messages or task launches are not the positive witness. - Function: attenuate inter-S1 competition for a bounded team&#39;s concurrent execution slots and conflicting simultaneous ownership of work transferred between model-running workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: oversubscribed independent member launches, duplicate/conflicting execution by old and successor agent on one unfinished delegated task, late/invalid lifecycle transitions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: team allocator admits at most bounded concurrent S1 member runs; handoff transition refuses successor execution until predecessor&#39;s side-effect execution barrier has closed. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party swarm coordinator/team lifecycle (C), not an autonomous S2 model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: FIFO excess-member queue, per-member lifecycle status, reserved handoff successor, cancellation and fail-closed timeout, mutex/condition synchronization. - Closure path: one S1 member requests spawn/handoff → team capacity or single-owner conflict is detected/enforced → member launch is deferred or incumbent stopped → queued/new agent executes only when legal → task status/results feed root and subsequent member assignments. - Boundary reachability: app.go ships tools.go in regular supported agent sessions; lifecycle.go / team.go implement coordination in the invoked swarm mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model decides which team actions to request, but capacity and conflicting dual-ownership attenuation remain hard-coded if that model is removed. - Evidence: tools.go; team.go; lifecycle.go; coordinator.go; app.go - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for resource/ownership coordination, not a general claim about detecting same-file edits across different tasks - Caveats: no general git-write/file-overlap conflict detector is credited. S2 claim is limited to specific execution-capacity and task-handoff interference, not universal multi-agent coordination.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: swarmstatus exposes per-task team, agent, current pending/running/failed/done/handoff state and aggregate counts, while swarmcollect returns all task results for the active swarm and mailbox messages provide targeted current feedback. - Current-control decision scope: the root model can change the present multi-S1 work allocation and follow-up by choosing additional spawns, targeted member messages, handoff to another agent type, or new work after collecting live results; this is distinct from the scheduler&#39;s fixed capacity policy. - Function: decide and revise current allocation of team coding commitments based on whole-active-task status and outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker failures, incomplete subtasks, uneven progress or current team capacity, requests requiring reassignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root model chooses spawned tasks, specialist roles, message/follow-up and live handoff based on team-wide status and returned outputs. - Decision owner: root model operating in optional first-party swarm mode, not the deterministic coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordinator task registry and statuses, swarmstatus, swarmcollect, swarmsend, swarmhandoff, policy inheritance and terminal status enforcement. - Closure path: root launches complementary S1 tasks → coordinator.go reports live roster/results → root selects current reassignment/message or correction → tools.go and lifecycle.go apply it to active member work → changed execution/status feeds next decisions. - Boundary reachability: real tool registration in app.go, available in shipped regular agent mode and not dependent on a custom third-party orchestrator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: hard-coded runtime records/executes tasks, but cannot decide the mission-relevant task split or discretionary correction after results without the root model. - Evidence: app.go; tools.go; coordinator.go; lifecycle.go; definition.go - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: an implementer S1&#39;s claim that its current code changes are correct, safe, and free of regressions/missing tests. - Ordinary reporting path: worker sends completion status and its own run/test output to root through a Task result or swarm coordinator; the implementing worker&#39;s self-report is not the complementary witness. - Complementary access path: the separately prompted built-in read-only code-review specialist has readfile/grep/glob/listdirectory access to actual workspace code/diffs and forms its own defect review independent of the implementer&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: dedicated model-run specialist with different reviewer system prompt and read-only tool category, invoked as an independent task context rather than reusing the implementer&#39;s own self-correction result. - Who acts on findings: the top-level S3 model can consume the returned reviewer findings and direct a worker to repair and optionally review again; no mandatory automatic blocking gate or independent human GitHub approval is claimed. - Function: independently inspect a coding worker&#39;s artifact for correctness defects, regressions, unsafe behavior or missing tests and return a separate diagnostic judgment to current-control decision making. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false or incomplete completion claims and regressions that may not be detected by the implementing S1. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party read-only code-review specialist model independently chooses which direct code/diff evidence to inspect and what actionable problems to report; root then chooses whether to correct/reassign. - Decision owner: separate specialist model in explicit review mode; corrective S3 follow-up remains under root&#39;s discretionary control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in review system prompt/read-only tool limit, Task subprocess/session, TaskOutput for asynchronous result, original source and diff read tools. - Closure path: root requests code review → separate model accesses current code/diff from the workspace → reports independent findings → Task/TaskOutput returns to root → root can ask an implementer to repair and re-review. - Boundary reachability: builtin.go ships code-review; tasktool.go dispatches it in supported main session; outputtool.go provides a result-return path, without external CI or human reviewer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic checks do not produce the separate semantic defect judgment; it is made by a model in a distinct read-only review context. - Evidence: builtin.go; tasktool.go; outputtool.go; SPECIALISTS.md; systemprompt.go; app.go - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: medium; optional invoked path, without measured production reviewer effectiveness - Caveats: not a mandatory final merge gate; parent can ignore findings and routine zero verify/self-correct is not credited as S3. This is assessment of reachable independent audit ownership, not benchmark-proven quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party future-oriented organizational capacity adaptation cycle is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: prospective changes in provider availability, future workflows and evolving capability requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no active owner shown deciding future organizational change and returning an applied capability revision. - Decision owner: user/developer config chooses external providers, plugins and specialist manifests; current agent handles current coding task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/provider selection, durable sessions, plan/context compaction, optional specialist generation, schedules and tools. - Closure path: current task tools/config are selected or generated, but no independently closed outside-and-then sensing → strategic adaptation → incorporated future capability loop at this recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-local plans, stored prompts and project-defined specialists do not by themselves establish S4. - Evidence: README.md; SPECIALISTS.md; systemprompt.go; app.go; scheduler.go; loop.go - Basis: explicit and structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no in-boundary ultimate-purpose/identity policy authority is established for the coding-agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts over constitutional purpose or organizational identity beyond per-command permission. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party owner able to set and bind the organization&#39;s ultimate mission/identity. - Decision owner: human/operator config sets task purpose and permission rules; main agent selects operations within those boundaries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool permission modes, sandbox, specialist capability restrictions, team policy inheritance and external user approvals. - Closure path: action is approved/denied by configured permissions, but no organizational identity conflict is escalated to an S5 owner and returned as a binding mission/policy change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: inherited permission/role policies constrain tool execution and cannot decide ultimate organizational purpose. - Evidence: README.md; SPECIALISTS.md; tasktool.go; team.go; app.go - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/26e21e5a1148a00686095ef2e113cc5d951e4fdc/assessments/zero.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 26e21e5a1148a00686095ef2e113cc5d951e4fdc\">26e21e5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T22:12:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shyftlabs/continuum\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Continuum</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shyftlabs/continuum/tree/e1ef4c45d08f81757972949b85670ca5453d7d0e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e1ef4c45d08f81757972949b85670ca5453d7d0e\">e1ef4c4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform goal-directed agent work through repeated contextual model decisions, Tool/handoff actions and returned environmental observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/application objective, model uncertainty, Tool/MCP results and errors, session/context state, handoff outcomes, provider responses and bounded execution conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose each substantive next answer, Tool call, handoff or continuation from current context and use returned observations to determine subsequent behavior. - Decision owner: the model-driven BaseAgent actor executed through Continuum&#39;s first-party AgentRunner/Executor path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run lifecycle/state, message construction, ToolHandler/ToolExecutor, handoff executor, max-turn/cycle guards, sessions/memory, policy/data-label enforcement, tracing and optional durable infrastructure. - Closure path: objective/context → first-party runner/executor → model decision → Tool/handoff or answer → first-party action execution and observation → observation appended to run context → next model decision or terminal state. - Boundary reachability: AgentRunner.run() is the documented quick-start and production API; the executor and Tool/handoff loop are directly wired into that supported path at the pinned revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime machinery transports, constrains and records the loop, but the substantive next-action decision is made by the model-driven agent over current context and available capabilities. External model endpoints supply inference but do not donate a separate organizational function beyond the actor Continuum directly invokes. - Evidence: runner.py; executor.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider internals, external MCP services and infrastructure are environmental dependencies and are not independently credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate scope overlap among sibling S1 workers that execute parallel slices of one Scatter workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: without partitioning, parallel workers can receive overlapping aspects of the same objective, duplicating work and producing competing/contradictory contributions for the shared gather stage. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a concrete distinct scope for every sibling worker such that the parallel assignments cover different items/aspects rather than overlap. - Decision owner: the autonomous splitter model invoked by the first-party ScatterAgent in its standard LLM-split mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: declared worker list, split prompt/schema, JSON parsing, branch-specific run contexts, concurrent dispatch, fail strategy and gather/merge stage. - Closure path: shared objective + sibling worker identities → Scatter split-model decision with explicit No overlap requirement → one selected slice is bound to each branch → each S1 executes on that slice → distinct results return into the shared gather stage. - Boundary reachability: LLM splitting is the default ScatterAgent path when explicit inputslices are not supplied; workflow agents are directly dispatched by AgentRunner through their execute() entry points. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime fixes the worker roster and parses/transports the split, but the model chooses the semantic separation relation from the current objective. Removing that model decision leaves only fallback same-input fan-out, which does not make the same adaptive no-overlap choice. - Evidence: scatter.py; runner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Parallel/DAG/Router/Handoff topology is not credited as S2 by itself. Explicit developer-supplied Scatter slices move the scope choice to configuration; the positive A state is established by the separately supported default model-split mode. - Distinct S1 units: two or more BaseAgent workers in one Scatter workflow, each directly executing a task slice through AgentRunner. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping assignments cause sibling workers to cover the same item/aspect, creating duplicated or potentially conflicting contributions to the same aggregate response; the built-in split prompt explicitly names overlap as the condition to avoid. - Attenuating coordination relation: a model-selected partition that must produce exactly one short, non-overlapping subtask for each declared worker. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each selected slice becomes that branch&#39;s actual runner.run(..., input=slice) input, so the coordination decision directly changes what each S1 does next. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is not merely fan-out or assignment; it is explicitly constructed to suppress a concrete cross-worker scope-overlap disturbance before concurrent operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current commitments of a Planner workflow organization by revising remaining work and specialist assignment in response to live execution results or failures. - Disturbance / variety regulated: completed outputs may invalidate later steps; a worker/step may fail; remaining commitments can cease to fit the goal/current state; specialist assignment may need replacement for unresolved work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether the remaining plan still makes sense and, when current feedback requires change, replace the remaining commitment set with a new sequence/agent assignment that the runtime will execute. - Decision owner: the autonomous planning/replanning model invoked by first-party PlannerAgent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current plan representation, completed-step history, failed-step/error capture, declared specialist pool, maxsteps, fail strategy, JSON parsing and deterministic execution of the accepted replacement plan. - Closure path: current goal + completed work + remaining commitments + latest output or failure/error → planner model judges CONTINUE or produces replacement steps → plansteps is replaced in the live workflow → subsequent worker dispatch follows the revised plan → new outcomes feed later current-control decisions. - Boundary reachability: PlannerAgent is a shipped workflow agent routed directly by AgentRunner; failure replanning is enabled by default (replanonfailure=True) and success-time replanning is an exposed first-party mode (enablereplanning=True). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes the returned plan and enforces limits, but it does not choose the new commitments. The model receives a whole-run current representation and decides whether/how to revise unresolved work; removing it leaves no equivalent discretionary replan decision. - Evidence: planner.py; runner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: initial goal decomposition, Router selection and static DAG scheduling are not credited as S3. The positive mapping relies on current execution feedback entering a whole-remaining-plan revision with authority to change subsequent commitments at the workflow/run recursion. - Whole-system current view: the replan path supplies the workflow goal, completed successful work, the failed/current step and error or last output, the complete remaining plan, and in pool mode the declared available agents. - Current-control decision scope: retain or replace unresolved steps, choose their instructions and (in agent-pool mode) their specialist assignments, thereby revising current organizational commitments after execution has begun.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party live-runtime path establishes sufficiently independent complementary audit of S1/S3 claims with corrective closure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Continuum supplies routine critique/scoring, traces and optional offline evaluation, but the supported runtime does not add an independent complementary evidence channel that challenges ordinary operational claims from materially different access to reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S3-specific audit judgment is established inside the declared live-runtime boundary. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reflection critique/retry, SupervisedSequential scoring/retry, decision traces/forkability, Langfuse observability, optional golden datasets/RAGAS/DeepEval evaluation and deterministic retry/gating logic. - Closure path: Reflection/Supervised findings can retry an operational stage, but they are routine same-path quality checks over ordinary task/output representations; offline evaluation can produce scores outside the live organization, but no standard runtime path returns an independent complementary audit finding into current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: separate model calls do make judgments, but independence of process alone is insufficient when the reviewer sees essentially the same operational report and is part of mandatory production QA. Optional offline evaluators are adjacent to the assessed runtime and do not close this function by repository co-location. - Evidence: reflection.py; supervised.py; README.md; src/continuum/evaluation/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an application could wire external ground truth or an independent evaluator into a Continuum organization; that downstream organization requires separate evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then prospective organizational adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: memory, model routing, Planner replanning and workflow reflection adapt current responses/execution to available context, but they do not model future environmental change and turn it into changed organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor is shown selecting a future-facing capability/organizational adaptation from external intelligence and returning it into present capability/S3. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: long-term semantic memory, intelligent-memory scoring/decay/entity/user profiles, session state, Planner replanning, Reflection retry, model routing/fallback, optional evaluation datasets and configurable Tools/policies. - Closure path: current/past context can influence later task execution, but no standard path closes external/future distinction → adaptation option → changed current capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model calls may plan, critique, remember and re-route, yet those decisions remain task/current-operation oriented. Persistence and learning-like memory do not supply the missing prospective organizational adaptation function. - Evidence: docs/memory.md; planner.py; reflection.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: applications can use Continuum primitives to build an S4 organization; generic composability is not C without an S4-specific first-party decision/feedback path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the workflow/run recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: policies, data labels, Tool trust/approval, model-routing rules and agent instructions constrain actions, but no path is shown deciding what the organization is or resolving identity-level policy tension through ultimate authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party runtime actor or parent path is shown receiving an identity/ultimate-policy issue, making the legitimate final decision and returning it to govern subsequent operation. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent instructions/config, policy stores, data-label propagation, Tool capability/trust controls, per-Tool human approval, security scanners, model-routing configuration and runtime enforcement. - Closure path: configured rules/approval declarations constrain individual actions and model/tool access; ordinary Tool approval returns a decision over one consequential action, not an identity/ultimate-policy decision for the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static or operator-supplied constraints do not own ultimate policy by being enforced, and the human approval handler is scoped to declared Tool calls rather than organizational identity. No autonomous or parent-governed S5 closure is established. - Evidence: approval.py; src/continuum/agent/config.py; executor.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream application can place identity authority around these enforcement primitives; that is a different system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c4afb9816e6066d91ae29683e802088b23fd46a2/assessments/continuum.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c4afb9816e6066d91ae29683e802088b23fd46a2\">c4afb98 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T15:27:26Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/EverMind-AI/Raven\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Raven</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/EverMind-AI/Raven/tree/e6c0344cb7ce00db25d554e4bb671ec1909a8f9f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e6c0344cb7ce00db25d554e4bb671ec1909a8f9f\">e6c0344 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform user/task objectives into answers, files, repository changes, research/design/oncall outputs or other tool-mediated outcomes through repeated model decisions and returned observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended task ambiguity; changing workspace/files; model/tool/MCP/plugin results; failed operations; child-agent outputs; conversation state and environmental feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool, response, delegation or task action after observing current context and prior results. - Decision owner: the model-driven Raven Host Agent for host work, or the model-driven built-in Raven child agent for its delegated operational task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Spine scheduling; Agent Loop; context engine; session persistence; tool registry; permissions; model-provider binding; child backends; DAG runner; cancellation and runtime limits. - Closure path: objective enters a supported entrance → Agent Loop assembles context/tools → model chooses response/tool/delegation → runtime executes/dispatches → observations return → model chooses the next action or completion → outcome persists or is delivered. - Boundary reachability: the Agent Loop is the documented standard execution path for the shipped host surfaces, and built-in agents are launched as supported Raven runtimes rather than examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model decision maker, scheduling and enforcement remain but open-ended substantive action selection is absent. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/architecture.md; docs-site/docs/agent-integrations.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: inference and some environmental actions can be supplied by external providers/tools; those dependencies do not own Raven&#39;s first-party operational loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate stale-write interference among concurrently operating Raven-Code S1 sessions sharing one repository checkout. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two Raven-Code sessions can read the same file, one can modify it, and the other can later attempt an edit based on stale content, risking overwrite/invalid work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether an edit may proceed against the version the session observed or must be refused because the content is unread/stale/externally changed. - Decision owner: constructor-defined Raven-Code code-flow/read-ledger policy; the model does not decide whether the stale-version guard fires. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-session read records; requirereadbeforeedit; live session ledger; workspace-concurrency notice; tool-error feedback to the agent. - Closure path: S1-A reads file → S1-B changes same shared file → S1-A requests edit against stale observation → first-party guard refuses write → refusal returns to S1-A → S1-A must re-read/replan/stop rather than silently overwrite B. - Boundary reachability: Raven-Code is shipped; its launcher enables the code-flow/tool configuration and the integration guide documents refusal for externally changed versions. - Distinct S1 units: separate Raven-Code sessions/instances execute independent coding tasks through their own model/tool loops while pointing at one checkout. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a write by one session invalidates another session&#39;s previously read content and can otherwise produce a stale/lost edit. - Attenuating coordination relation: the read-version precondition blocks the stale edit at the first-party file-tool boundary; live concurrency notices additionally expose the shared-directory condition. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the refused edit is returned to the affected Raven-Code loop, which can re-read current state, narrow work or stop/report the conflict. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness detects a concrete cross-S1 write disturbance and prevents one stale action from entering the shared environment; it is not merely messaging, DAG order or delegation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination choice is fixed by configured tool policy rather than selected by a model after reasoning over inter-worker state, so the state is C. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/agent-integrations.md; agents/raven-code/plugins/code-flow/codeflow/config.py; agents/raven-code/plugins/code-flow/codeflow/flow.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is a bounded S2 witness, not general workspace isolation. Raven documents that shared checkouts have no universal file locks/worktrees and full writes are not universally guarded.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain current control over an active delegated organization by inspecting live DAG/worker/task state and changing ongoing commitments through continuation, replanning, abandonment, cancellation or targeted steering. - Disturbance / variety regulated: delegated nodes can be pending/running/completed/exceptional/failed/skipped/cancelled/interrupted; outputs can be incomplete; dependencies can block; current work may need correction or retirement. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which current delegated work continues, receives corrective instructions, is replanned/replaced, is abandoned/cancelled, or is steered while running. - Decision owner: base A mode — Host Agent using first-party DAG/status/control tools; parent P mode — authorized user/control client using first-party task/instance inspection plus direct instance turns or supported steering. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DAG run/node records; dagstatus; resolvedagnode; canceldag; instance history; direct instance chat; steering RPC; foreground/background lifecycle. - Closure path: workers execute → Raven records statuses/results/exceptions → current-control owner inspects run/instances → owner selects intervention → runtime applies it → later execution proceeds under the updated commitment. - Boundary reachability: DAG controls are part of shipped orchestration; task/instance inspection and steering are exposed by first-party collaboration/RPC surfaces. - Whole-system current view: DAG status and persisted graph state expose the current node set/run status to the host; first-party task/instance views expose active delegated work to the parent controller. - Current-control decision scope: intervention changes current worker instructions or commitments rather than only enforcing a fixed semaphore, timeout or permission rule. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: base mode is model-selected current-control after reading current evidence; the parent path is a separate supported ownership mode over the same ongoing organization. - Evidence: docs-site/docs/orchestration.md; docs-site/docs/agent-collaboration.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for base mode; medium-high for parent mode. - Caveats: steering support is backend-dependent; the parent witness is the supported Raven instance/control path, not every external backend.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently audit a Raven-Code session&#39;s repository-side completion/integration claims against machine-read Git reality and expose blockers/attribution to the host. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a coding agent can claim completion while the workspace has uncommitted changes, no commits past the pinned base, unreadable Git state, or changes that cannot be attributed to that session because another session shared the directory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine manifest status from independently read repository facts rather than the worker&#39;s prose. - Decision owner: constructor-defined Raven-Code manifest logic; status calculation is deterministic, not chosen by the coding model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-session base commit; Git reads for HEAD/branch/status/log/diff; shared-session ledger; fail-closed blockers; ACP meta publication. - Closure path: Raven-Code works and returns ordinary model response → archive hook separately reads Git/workspace state → manifest logic computes attribution/status/blockers → report is attached to ACP metadata → Host Agent/user can use independent facts for integration/continuation/escalation. - Boundary reachability: Raven-Code&#39;s shipped code-flow hook calls buildmanifest() on normal turn archive and publishes raven.harnessManifest through ACP metadata. - Claim being audited: whether the coding session produced attributable repository changes that are in a complete, integration-ready Git state. - Ordinary reporting path: Raven-Code model&#39;s natural-language completion response and checklist/task claims. - Complementary access path: direct Git/worktree measurement from the first-party code-flow hook; manifest fields are machine-read and explicitly not sourced from model prose. - Independence boundary: the coding model does not choose manifest values; unknown/shared evidence becomes a blocker rather than accepting the worker claim. This is complementary runtime evidence, not an external trust domain. - Who acts on findings: Host Agent or authorized user/controller receiving ACP metadata decides whether to integrate, continue, reassign or investigate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit verdict is deterministic runtime policy, so C; downstream management ownership does not transfer ownership of the audit decision itself. - Evidence: manifest.py; flow.py; agent-integrations.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the manifest audits repository state/attribution, not arbitrary semantic code correctness; model-based DAG verdicts and optional Eval Engine are not separately credited as S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert external evaluation of Raven worker behaviour into a prospective persistent Harness revision that changes the worker&#39;s future operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated mismatch between desired behaviour/requirements and observed worker behaviour or deliverables across rounds can persist even when the current task finishes; the organization needs a path that learns from those external distinctions and changes how later work is performed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether observed signals justify curation and, when they do, choose the concrete Harness mechanism/revision to install for the worker&#39;s next rounds. - Decision owner: a first-party autonomous RSI path split across model-driven actors: the Analyst makes the semantic curate / continue / supplement / clarify / stop feedback decision from evaluator signals and execution evidence, and the Curator chooses the concrete adaptation across the worker Harness. Deterministic code validates, installs and records that decision but does not make the semantic adaptation judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Trial/Evaluator protocols; Signal and execution/activity records; bounded Analyst exchange; Curator understand/select/design/implement/repair stages; host-owned Harness declarations/contracts; revision validation; persistent worker process; native Raven extension points; iteration state/records. - Closure path: worker performs a Trial → evaluator returns external Signals about actual behaviour/deliverables → Analyst interprets those signals plus execution evidence and can decide curate → Curator designs a persistent Harness revision → workflow.improve validates and installs it → a later Trial runs the same worker organization through the real Raven AgentLoop with the revised Harness → new behaviour is evaluated again. - Boundary reachability: Raven documents this generic RSI mode in the pinned source distribution, ships experimental/curator, experimental/analyst, and experimental/iteration, exposes a source-checkout uv run python -m experimental.iteration entry path, and binds generated strategies through Raven&#39;s native AgentLoop extension points. The mode is explicitly experimental and not included in the installed wheel, but it is a first-party documented source-distribution operating mode rather than a repository-development benchmark actor borrowed from evolver/. - External distinction: evaluation originates outside the worker&#39;s ordinary production loop: a human, dataset or scenario evaluator inspects the worker&#39;s conversations/deliverables against requirements or withheld criteria and returns Signals; evaluator-private criteria are not written by the worker or Curator. - Future / prospective distinction: the Analyst converts current-round evidence into behavioural requirements for subsequent rounds, and the Curator modifies persistent Harness strategy/configuration so later work is performed under a changed capability rather than merely retrying the same current action. - Adaptation option generated: the Curator model can select and generate revisions across Raven&#39;s Harness strategy surfaces (Memory, Planning, Capability and Action) and connected home/config/hook/plugin/child-Harness extension points, then repair a candidate until it satisfies the host contract or fails validation. - Path back into current capability / S3: workflow.improve validates and activates the selected revision in the worker, and the next Trial exercises that revision through the real Raven AgentLoop; subsequent operational evidence therefore reflects the changed present capability and can enter another adaptation round. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven Analyst/Curator actors while leaving Trial plumbing, validation, installation and persistence intact removes the semantic decision that a behaviour gap warrants curation and the choice of what organizational capability to change. The deterministic machinery enforces the selected revision but does not reproduce materially the same discretionary adaptation choice, so the supported RSI mode is A rather than C. - Evidence: README.md; CONTEXT.md; experimental/README.md; experimental/docs/rsi-iteration.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the documented source-distribution experimental mode; this does not claim the ordinary wheel-install mode autonomously self-evolves. - Caveats: the S4 witness is explicitly experimental and source-checkout-only at the reviewed revision. Scenario-specific experimental/simulation/ actors and the standalone evolver/ are not imported as owners; they are unnecessary to establish the generic Analyst/Curator/Iteration closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish durable identity/behavior and non-overridable operating policy within which the Raven organization may act. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/tool behavior can drift across tasks; tools can request unsafe/disallowed actions; specialized agents require stable identity/conduct across sessions and launches. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define/edit Agent-home identity/conduct files and configure persistent allow/ask/deny rules/permission mode that constrain subsequent tool dispatch. - Decision owner: legitimate operator/user maintaining Raven Agent home and configuration; no first-party autonomous agent is established as ultimate authority over these controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agentmemory/profile/soul.md; agentmemory/profile/agent.md; context bootstrap assembly; launcher seeding/preservation of operator edits; Permission Gate; persistent tool rules; built-in denials. - Closure path: operator edits identity/conduct/policy → runtime/launcher preserves files/config → context assembler injects identity/behavior into later model calls and Permission Gate evaluates later tool calls → subsequent operation occurs inside returned policy boundary. - Boundary reachability: bootstrap identity files are live standard context inputs; shipped agent launchers preserve operator edits; permission rules are evaluated before first-party tool dispatch. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what the agent is instructed to be/how it must behave, and which classes of tool actions are permitted, require approval or are forbidden even when a model wants to execute them. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: in the claimed parent-governed mode, the operator/user controlling Agent home and Raven configuration is the legitimate ultimate authority; constructor built-in denials remain above ordinary model discretion and no autonomous agent override path is established. - Return-to-operation path: changed identity files are read into future system context; changed permission rules/mode are applied to future tool decisions, governing later operational turns. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Raven agents operate under these identity/policy inputs but no supported autonomous path grants them legitimate final authority to rewrite or bypass operator identity and deny rules. - Evidence: render.py; agents/README.md; permissions.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: session mode changes and smart permission review can alter ordinary operation but do not supersede user deny rules or constructor denials; generic per-action approval is not itself the S5 witness.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4f43158d9ef9986ef60818af6b69002c54f6aa6d/assessments/raven.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4f43158d9ef9986ef60818af6b69002c54f6aa6d\">4f43158 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T19:16:41+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/exoharness/exo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/exoharness/exo/tree/6164288895a5851b2118492c880fb37b543e2ac6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6164288895a5851b2118492c880fb37b543e2ac6\">6164288 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform open-ended research/operations work on the local-machine/external environment through repeated model decisions and first-party tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user or adapter objectives, changing sandbox/filesystem state, web/service responses, command/tool outcomes and failures, scheduled wakeups, and newly observed information during a turn. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose each next tool/action or final response from the current prompt and returned observations. - Decision owner: the running Exo model actor inside the first-party responses turn loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: exoharness durable context/events, prompt assembly, tool registry/execution, sandbox, scheduler/adapters, provider runtime and configured round limits. - Closure path: model decision → first-party tool execution or external action → result/event appended to canonical context → next model round incorporates the observation and revises action until completion. - Boundary reachability: exo/harness.ts directly invokes the shipped runResponsesHarnessTurn in the canonical harness; no adjacent development agent or downstream orchestration is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code supplies context, transport and execution, but the model actor selects discretionary next actions in response to changing observations. - Evidence: pinned exo/harness.ts and exoharness/typescript/model-runtime/turn-loop.ts; README corroborates the canonical long-running Agent operating boundary. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: available tools, configured limits and provider behavior constrain operational variety without replacing the Agent&#39;s next-action discretion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 relation among distinct sibling S1 units is established in the frozen standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying inter-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation with an implemented attenuation loop was found. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific coordination discretion is supplied at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: conversation/event ordering, scheduler timing, adapter supervision, sandbox scoping, conversation forks and lineage-related state primitives. - Closure path: no qualifying coordination-result→subsequent-sibling-S1 feedback loop is implemented at the assessed boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the frozen implementation establishes one primary long-running Agent plus tasks/adapters/conversations, not an implemented sibling multi-Agent organization with a concrete interference-control relation. - Evidence: pinned README.md, especially its ongoing-work statement for high-level multi-agent orchestration, and docs/SELF-CONTROL.md, which marks whole-agent cloning/migration as not yet built. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: README language about recursive improvement/lineage describes architectural direction and primitives; it is not sufficient to manufacture an S2 function that implementation-specific material says is unfinished.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the currently running Exo installation&#39;s host-side service capacity and lifecycle on behalf of the whole. - Disturbance / variety regulated: scheduler/adapter process failure or stale state, need to stop/restart current host services, fresh-build activation, and mismatch between current process state and desired installation-wide service state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect current guardian-managed service status/logs and choose start/stop/restart/rebuild actions for scheduler and adapter runners, including the control REPL refresh path. - Decision owner: the self-hosted human operator in the supported guardian/./exo.sh parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PID/state files, graceful drain markers, process supervision, build scripts, reboot notices and preserved .exo state. - Closure path: operator observes the whole guardian-managed current service state → invokes the chosen guardian control → deterministic supervisor drains/stops/starts/rebuilds affected services → later current operation continues under the selected service state. - Boundary reachability: exo/scripts/exo-service-guardian and ./exo.sh are documented standard day-to-day operating surfaces in the canonical self-hosted distribution; they are not contributor-only development utilities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Agent receives read-only/introspection and a narrow self-update handoff, but maintainer instructions explicitly keep service status, host logs and targeted service controls as operator CLI responsibilities. Thus the whole-installation current-control decision is parent-owned. - Evidence: pinned exo/scripts/exo-service-guardian, exo/SELF.md, exo/harness.ts, docs/SELF-CONTROL.md and README operating instructions. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: conversation-local task/adapter management and the fixed rebuildandrestartexo activation tool are not separately credited as autonomous S3 because they do not establish an autonomous whole-installation current-control view/right. - Whole-system current view: guardian status reports scheduler and adapter runner status plus pending REPL restart state; standard operating material identifies it as the host-service control surface for the installation. - Current-control decision scope: build and start/stop/restart of the scheduler and adapter service capacity, including current service recovery while preserving durable state.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no complete first-party complementary-audit function with sufficiently independent operational access and audit judgment is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no S3-specific claim/risk is closed by an independent audit relation at the declared boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent audit judgment/feedback right is supplied. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only canonical event history, listconversationevents, adapter telemetry, git history, guardian outcome records and host logs. - Closure path: these records can support diagnosis and reconstruction, but no distinct auditor observes operational reality through a sufficiently independent path, forms findings and returns those findings through a credited current-control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Exo gives the same Agent unusually durable visibility into what happened to it, including evidence surviving sandbox rewind, but protected observability/history is not by itself complementary audit under the Profile. - Evidence: pinned docs/RSI.md, docs/SELF-CONTROL.md, exo/SELF.md and exo/tools/introspection-tools.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the immutable event log is stronger evidence than ordinary ephemeral tracing and is useful audit substrate; the negative mapping is about the missing independent audit function/judgment/closure, not a claim that Exo lacks traceability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: turn changing external capability demands and observed operational limitations into reusable future capability changes to the running Exo harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new user/environment requirements, missing tools/integrations, recurring work that deserves durable capability, failures or limitations exposed by real operation, and future-fit concerns in prompts/tooling/harness behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — the Exo Agent decides what reusable tool, skill, prompt or source-level harness adaptation to implement and whether/how to validate and activate it; parent mode — the operator decides the adaptation and directly edits/configures the corresponding first-party capability before rebuilding/restarting it. - Decision owner: base (A) — the long-running Exo model actor using its intentionally exposed self-maintenance/capability-extension surfaces; parent (P) — the self-hosted human operator through direct source/prompt/configuration edits and standard activation controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source mount and self-map, shell, managetool, skills, sandbox snapshots/rewind, git history, tests/build commands, rebuildandrestartexo, durable update events and guardian service activation. - Closure path: base — externally observed need/limitation → Agent develops a reusable adaptation → writes/installs it → validates and, for source changes, invokes the guardian build/restart → later turns operate with the changed capability. Parent — operator forms the adaptation judgment → edits the same capability surface → rebuild/restart or normal reload → subsequent Agent operation uses the changed capability. - Boundary reachability: self-source visibility, managed-tool creation and the fixed guardian self-update path are deliberately wired into the canonical standard Agent prompt/toolset; the parent edit/guardian path is also a supported day-to-day self-hosted mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: code mounting, git and guardian are supporting machinery; in base mode the model actor can itself interpret an externally revealed capability gap, choose the future reusable change and activate it without requiring a developer to compose an additional adaptation actor or closure loop. - Evidence: pinned README, docs/RSI.md, docs/SELF-CONTROL.md, exo/harness.ts, exo/SELF.md, exo/tools/guardian-tools.ts and managed-tool paths documented at the frozen ref. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic source-code mutability alone would not be S4. The credited witness is the standard long-running Agent&#39;s externally coupled path from encountered capability demand/limitation to a reusable tool/prompt/harness adaptation that is returned into later capability. Roadmap-only autonomous periodic cleanup is not used as evidence. - External distinction: a user/environment exposes a capability need, failed/insufficient current behavior, integration requirement or recurring operational pattern that the existing harness does not adequately absorb. - Future / prospective distinction: the Agent distinguishes a one-off task from a reusable capability change intended to alter later turns—installed tools are explicitly re-registered on following model rounds, skills persist across conversations, and source/prompt updates are activated for future operation. - Adaptation option generated: create/replace a managed tool; install a durable reusable skill; change prompts/tool schemas/harness source; add an adapter/integration; or modify broader Exo code and validate/restart it. - Path back into current capability / S3: managed-tool changes become callable on a later model round; skills/prompt changes enter later context; source edits become running code after the first-party validation/build/restart guardian path. Parent guardian control supplies the current-service activation edge without owning the base Agent&#39;s adaptation judgment.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: govern the durable identity, purpose and highest-level operating policy of the Exo Agent through the first-party prompt/harness surfaces that define subsequent behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tension between the Agent&#39;s current durable identity/operating principles and a proposed new purpose, ethos or ultimate behavior policy, whether the proposal arises from the Agent&#39;s own self-evolution judgment or from its parent operator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — decide to revise durable identity/operating-rule surfaces such as exo/prompts/me.md or equivalent harness-policy assembly and activate that revision; parent mode — authoritatively choose and edit those same identity/policy surfaces for the local installation. - Decision owner: base (A) — the Exo model actor, which is intentionally granted the standard-distribution self-source write/activation path over its identity/harness policy; parent (P) — the self-hosted user/operator, who can directly define/edit the durable identity/prompt policy and activate or override it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mounted source repository, shell, exo/SELF.md, git history/revert, prompt assembly in exo/harness.ts, the fixed guardian build/restart operation and operator service controls. - Closure path: base — identity/ultimate-policy issue is judged by the Agent → Agent edits the durable identity/policy source → validates/commits as appropriate and invokes the first-party rebuild/restart path → subsequent turns receive the changed authoritative prompt/policy. Parent — operator makes the identity/policy decision → direct first-party-supported prompt/source edit and activation → subsequent Agent turns are governed by it. - Boundary reachability: the canonical prompt tells Exo that its own source tree is mounted and that it should inspect/modify itself through SELF.md plus rebuildandrestartexo; docs/SELF-CONTROL.md identifies prompt evolution as an existing self-control capability, and README separately documents direct human prompt editing. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the positive base claim is not inferred from the existence of a static system prompt. The standard Agent is the actor that can inspect and alter the durable identity/operating-policy artifact and close the change into its own later runtime; no additional developer-composed S5 actor is required. The supported human path remains a distinct parent-authority mode and can override the autonomous configuration. - Evidence: pinned exo/prompts/me.md, exo/harness.ts, exo/SELF.md, docs/RSI.md, docs/SELF-CONTROL.md, README and exo/tools/guardian-tools.ts. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: normal task instructions, adapter side-effect rules and tool permissions are not individually promoted to S5. A(P) is limited to the documented durable identity/basic-harness-policy self-modification path plus the distinct operator-owned path; it does not imply that every runtime constraint is autonomously mutable. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: exo/prompts/me.md explicitly states what the Agent is, its purpose and broad operating rules; maintainer material calls it the durable identity prompt and states that prompt/basic-harness policy can evolve through the self-modification path. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: base (A) — the standard Exo Agent can itself select and activate a change to its durable identity/basic harness policy without a separate approval mechanism in that mode; parent (P) — the local operator can authoritatively set/override those surfaces and activate the resulting build/configuration. - Return-to-operation path: identity/policy source enters exoInstructions on later turns; source-level changes are activated through rebuildandrestartexo or the operator guardian path, after which subsequent operation loads the changed policy.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7201c5dbcc2e67e8dad6e1354475c8738bb40d6e/assessments/exo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7201c5dbcc2e67e8dad6e1354475c8738bb40d6e\">7201c5d &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
